e final project.","duration_minutes":120,"objectives":["Explain the difference between boosting a post and creating an ad in Meta Ads Manager, and give an overview of Google Ads","Choose an ad objective, audience, creative and budget for a small campaign","Build a campaign budget in KES covering content, data, ads and other costs, with a cost-per-result estimate","Explain reach, impressions, engagement rate, clicks, messages, conversions, cost per result and return on ad spend","Read basic insights on Facebook, Instagram or WhatsApp Business and track offline results with simple codes","Assemble a complete campaign plan with a SMART objective, KPIs and a timeline","Improve the campaign based on peer testing"],"topics":["Developing a practical digital marketing strategy","Digital advertising fundamentals","Marketing analytics and measuring campaign performance"],"activities":["Recap and results from your first post","Introduction to online advertising","Campaign planning and budgeting in KES","Marketing analytics and measuring results","Stretch break","Campaign build","Peer testing and revision","Showcase preparation and homework"],"expected_output":"Complete campaign plan (all twelve sections); Campaign budget in KES with cost-per-result estimate (or zero-budget time plan); KPI list with targets and a tracking sheet; Peer-test feedback and a revision list","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.2 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management. DIRA approval of 8 Nov 2026 (Digital Marketing Sessions 5-7, topics and practical outputs): this session carries the approved topics \"Digital advertising fundamentals\", \"Marketing analytics and measuring campaign performance\" and \"Developing a practical digital marketing strategy\" and the approved output \"digital marketing portfolio project\" (built here as the campaign plan) with practice of the \"three-minute walk-through\" (all approved Session 7). This V13.2 record itself is a draft awaiting session approval.","is_approved":false,"source":"curriculum_v13"}, {"id":"f067f3b7-67fc-4a7d-aa5b-33657e3e61b1","programme_id":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000000a1","track_id":"0304aafe-1338-465c-a0fc-b756a476a310","week_no":2,"session_no":4,"title":"Target audiences, social media marketing and WhatsApp Business","description":"Define target audiences and customer personas, use Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn for marketing, and set up WhatsApp Business to serve and sell to customers.","duration_minutes":120,"objectives":["Explain target audience, segmentation and customer persona with examples","Build a customer persona from real customer answers (demographics, needs, problems, habits, where they spend time online)","Compare Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn by audience, content formats and best uses for marketing","Explain in simple terms how feeds decide what people see (relevance, engagement, consistency)","Set up or plan a WhatsApp Business profile with a catalogue, greeting, away message, quick replies and labels","Use broadcast lists and status responsibly, with customer consent","Choose the platforms and posting frequency for their campaign"],"topics":["Social media marketing (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn)","WhatsApp Business"],"activities":["Recap and customer answers","Target audiences and customer personas","Build your customer persona","Social media marketing on four platforms","Stretch break","Demonstration and practice - WhatsApp Business","Platform plan for your campaign","Knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Customer persona based on real customer information; WhatsApp Business plan (or a set-up profile on a business number); Platform plan with formats, posting times and reasons","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.2 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management. DIRA approval of 8 Nov 2026 (Digital Marketing Sessions 5-7, topics and practical outputs): this session carries the approved topics \"Social media marketing (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn)\" (from approved Session 5) and \"WhatsApp Business\" and the approved output \"WhatsApp Business profile plan\" (both from approved Session 6). This V13.2 record itself is a draft awaiting session approval.","is_approved":false,"source":"curriculum_v13"}, {"id":"f0b76394-6bbd-4eb6-83d8-40ce47578278","programme_id":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000000a1","track_id":null,"week_no":1,"session_no":1,"title":"Self-leadership, confidence and wellbeing","description":"Know your strengths and values, start your personal brand, set a four-week goal and build habits that keep you well and confident at work.","duration_minutes":110,"objectives":["Describe at least three personal strengths, two interests and two values, with an example of each from real life","Write a first-draft personal brand statement (two sentences) based on strengths, values and the help they can offer others","Set one SMART four-week learning goal and name the first step they will take this week","Practise three confident workplace behaviours (introducing yourself, asking for help, accepting feedback)","Identify two wellbeing habits and one support route they can use during the programme","Explain the programme expectations, the learning agreement and how to raise a safeguarding concern"],"topics":[],"activities":["Welcome and learning agreement","Icebreaker - two truths and a strength","Values, confidence and self-leadership mini-workshop","Strengths map and personal brand statement","Confidence at work - practice rounds","Wellbeing and resilience","SMART goal and accountability partner","Reflection and exit ticket"],"expected_output":"Completed strengths map (strengths, interests, values, help I can offer); First-draft personal brand statement (two sentences); Goal card with a SMART four-week goal, first step and two wellbeing habits; Accountability partner agreed; Baseline self-assessment and exit ticket submitted","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.2 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management.","is_approved":false,"source":"curriculum_v13"}]$dira_json$::jsonb); alter table public.curriculum_sessions enable trigger user;
alter table public.connect_retention_settings disable trigger user; insert into public.connect_retention_settings(id, enabled, months, updated_by, updated_at) select id, enabled, months, updated_by, updated_at from jsonb_populate_recordset(null::public.connect_retention_settings, $dira_json$[{"id":true,"enabled":false,"months":12,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:47.511926+03:00"}]$dira_json$::jsonb); alter table public.connect_retention_settings enable trigger user;
alter table public.curriculum_lesson_plans disable trigger user; insert into public.curriculum_lesson_plans(id, session_id, version, content, status, approved_by, approved_at, updated_by, updated_at, created_at) select id, session_id, version, content, status, approved_by, approved_at, updated_by, updated_at, created_at from jsonb_populate_recordset(null::public.curriculum_lesson_plans, $dira_json$[{"id":"1a96fd24-c4c1-421a-b98d-39c842ff3836","session_id":"8d817596-1982-4768-9f6f-509ec448a925","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["AI chat assistant, Google Sheets, Google Forms", "Screenshots of a no-code automation tool (optional)", "Workflow map template and prompt set template", "Free tools only: Google Sheets and Forms (free with a Google account); workflow maps on paper; automation shown through screenshots - no paid tool is needed."], "topics": [{"title": "AI for productivity and everyday tasks", "subtopics": ["Planning a week or a project", "Summarising notes and meetings you have permission to record", "To-do lists and priorities", "Emails and messages", "Spreadsheet formulas and simple data analysis", "Studying and job searching"]}, {"title": "AI-assisted business activities", "subtopics": ["Customer replies and FAQs", "Product descriptions", "Simple market and competitor research (verified)", "Social media post ideas", "Drafting business documents (quotes, invoices, simple plans)", "Where AI should not decide (pricing without data, hiring, credit)"]}, {"title": "Introduction to automation", "subtopics": ["Trigger, action and human check", "Automation already in everyday tools (auto-replies, filters, scheduled posts, forms to sheets)", "No-code automation tools - overview", "When automation goes wrong", "Keeping a human in the loop"]}, {"title": "Privacy, bias and limitations of AI", "subtopics": ["Personal data and consent", "Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 - fair, lawful, limited use", "Tool settings and data retention", "Bias - where it comes from and examples", "Language and local-context gaps (Kiswahili, Sheng, local languages)", "Over-reliance and skills", "When not to use AI"]}], "purpose": "Learners apply AI to routine work - planning, summarising, organising, customer communication and business tasks - and learn how simple automations save time. They study privacy, bias and the limits of AI in depth, and design a workflow with reusable prompts and clear human checks.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A Thika salon using AI-drafted, owner-approved replies for booking questions and a WhatsApp away message after hours.", "A Nairobi youth group using a Google Form and Sheet to register 200 participants for a career fair.", "Image tools showing stereotyped pictures of 'a doctor' or 'a farmer in Africa'.", "An AI chatbot that promised a free delivery the business does not offer."], "homework": ["Finalise your project plan and gather everything you need for the build (content, information from your user, prompt library).", "Arrange for one real user to test your solution during or right after session 7. If no real user is available, or testing would involve their personal data, test with a classmate using invented sample data."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and time audit", "minutes": 10, "summary": "List the routine tasks that take up your time each week."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "AI for productivity and everyday tasks", "minutes": 15, "summary": "See practical ways AI saves time on planning, summarising, organising and spreadsheets."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "AI-assisted business activities", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn how small businesses use AI for customer replies, descriptions, research and content, and where AI should not decide."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Introduction to automation", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Understand trigger-action automation and spot safe opportunities in a workflow."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Privacy, bias and limitations", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how to protect personal data, recognise bias and know the limits of AI."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Workflow map and reusable prompt set", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Map a routine workflow, mark AI, automation and human steps, and build a reusable prompt set."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Answer questions on productivity, automation, privacy and bias."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What are the three parts of an automation?"}, {"question": "Name two tasks where AI should not make the final decision."}, {"question": "What does the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 require when handling personal data?"}, {"question": "Give one example of AI bias and how to reduce it."}, {"question": "How do you anonymise data before using AI?"}], "objectives": ["Use AI for at least four productivity tasks (planning, summarising, organising, drafting, spreadsheet help)", "Use AI for small business activities (customer replies, product descriptions, simple market research, social posts) with checks", "Explain automation (trigger, action, human check) and identify three automation opportunities in a workflow", "Explain how personal data must be protected when using AI, with reference to Kenya's Data Protection Act 2019", "Identify bias and limitations in AI outputs, including language and local-context gaps", "Map a routine workflow and build a reusable prompt set with human checkpoints"], "track_project": "An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent.", "expected_outputs": ["Workflow map with H, AI and A steps, data points and human checks", "Reusable prompt set (tested)", "Updated prompt library"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Privacy review", "instructions": "Review a partner's workflow map for privacy risks and missing human checks."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Bias spotting", "instructions": "Ask an image or text tool for 'a picture/description of a successful entrepreneur' and discuss whose image appears and who is missing."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Entrepreneurship & Business teaches business processes and decisions; AI Tools shows where AI and automation can support them and where humans must decide.", "Digital Marketing uses WhatsApp Business features as marketing tools; AI Tools uses them as examples of simple automation."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Workflow map", "instructions": "Map a routine workflow with H, AI and A labels, personal data points and human checks."}, {"title": "Reusable prompt set", "instructions": "Write three to five placeholder prompts for the workflow and test at least two."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"25e48b1f-0673-47b1-843d-eddf8cecf224","session_id":"f067f3b7-67fc-4a7d-aa5b-33657e3e61b1","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Persona template and platform plan template", "WhatsApp Business app on a test phone, or prepared screenshots", "Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn (viewing examples)", "Homework answers from customers"], "topics": [{"title": "Target audiences and segmentation", "subtopics": ["Who is your customer (not everyone)", "Segmenting by age, location, income, interests and needs", "Primary and secondary audiences", "Using real customer conversations"]}, {"title": "Customer personas", "subtopics": ["Name, age and situation", "Goals and problems", "Buying habits and budget", "Online habits", "Objections and what builds trust"]}, {"title": "Social media marketing on Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn", "subtopics": ["Facebook - pages, groups and Marketplace", "Instagram - posts, reels, stories, highlights", "TikTok - short video, trends, sounds and lives", "LinkedIn - professional services, B2B and careers", "Business and creator accounts and their insights", "How feeds choose content"]}, {"title": "WhatsApp Business", "subtopics": ["Business profile", "Catalogue and prices", "Greeting and away messages", "Quick replies", "Labels for orders", "Status for updates", "Broadcast lists and consent", "Professional response times"]}], "purpose": "Learners stop marketing to 'everyone'. They define a target audience and build a customer persona from real information, learn what each major social platform is best for and how its content is shown to people, and set up a WhatsApp Business profile with a catalogue, greeting and quick replies. They leave with a persona and a platform plan for their campaign.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Shiru's Bakes persona 'Mercy, 32, accountant in Juja'.", "A Kisumu photographer who books graduation shoots through Instagram highlights and a WhatsApp link.", "A Nairobi CV-writing freelancer who finds clients on LinkedIn by posting before-and-after CV tips.", "A Kakamega poultry farmer who sends weekly egg prices to a broadcast list of 80 customers who saved his number.", "A mitumba trader in Gikomba who sells through TikTok lives in the evening."], "homework": ["Interview two more real customers or potential customers to test your persona and update it.", "Follow three Kenyan businesses on your main platform and note what they post, when, and what gets the most engagement.", "If the business owner agrees, set up or improve their WhatsApp Business profile."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and customer answers", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share what customers said about how they found the business."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Target audiences and customer personas", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how to choose a specific audience and describe them as a persona."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Build your customer persona", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Create a persona for your campaign using the template and real customer answers."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Social media marketing on four platforms", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Compare Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn and how each shows content to people."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Demonstration and practice - WhatsApp Business", "minutes": 20, "summary": "See a WhatsApp Business profile set up step by step, then plan or set up your own."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Platform plan for your campaign", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Choose your campaign platforms, formats and posting times based on your persona."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Answer short questions on personas, platforms and WhatsApp Business."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Why is 'everyone' not a good target audience for a small business?"}, {"question": "Name four parts of a customer persona."}, {"question": "Which platform would you choose for a B2B cleaning company and why?"}, {"question": "Name three WhatsApp Business features that save time."}, {"question": "What must you do before adding a customer to a broadcast list or group?"}], "objectives": ["Explain target audience, segmentation and customer persona with examples", "Build a customer persona from real customer answers (demographics, needs, problems, habits, where they spend time online)", "Compare Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn by audience, content formats and best uses for marketing", "Explain in simple terms how feeds decide what people see (relevance, engagement, consistency)", "Set up or plan a WhatsApp Business profile with a catalogue, greeting, away message, quick replies and labels", "Use broadcast lists and status responsibly, with customer consent", "Choose the platforms and posting frequency for their campaign"], "track_project": "A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission.", "expected_outputs": ["Customer persona based on real customer information", "WhatsApp Business plan (or a set-up profile on a business number)", "Platform plan with formats, posting times and reasons"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Persona questions", "instructions": "Swap personas with a partner who asks two questions it does not answer yet; update your persona."}, {"title": "Platform plan challenge", "instructions": "Present your platform plan to two peers, who check it against your persona."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Segment and choose", "instructions": "List three customer segments for your brief and choose one primary segment with a reason."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Entrepreneurship & Business also defines customers and target markets, to decide whether a business idea is worth building. Digital Marketing builds personas to decide channels, messages and timing for an existing offer."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Customer persona", "instructions": "Complete the persona template with at least two facts from real customer answers."}, {"title": "WhatsApp Business plan", "instructions": "Write the profile description, three catalogue items with prices, greeting and away messages, three quick replies and three labels."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"2decd90e-e33f-4685-8625-b8035390b3aa","session_id":"810c6163-429e-4db5-b72a-b88bfd6e88cd","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Canva (free) or a similar design tool; paper and pens as a backup", "CRAP checklist, brand kit template, credit and consent log template, simple model release template", "Free stock and Creative Commons sources (follow their licence terms)", "Free design options: Canva free, the phone's built-in markup tools, or a paper layout photographed in good light; design offline where possible."], "topics": [{"title": "Graphic design fundamentals", "subtopics": ["Contrast", "Repetition", "Alignment", "Proximity", "Typography - two fonts, sizes and weights", "Colour - palettes and meaning", "Grids and hierarchy", "Readable on a phone"]}, {"title": "Visual storytelling", "subtopics": ["Carousels as stories", "Before-and-after", "Step-by-step", "Simple infographics", "Thumbnails and covers", "Posters for events and causes"]}, {"title": "Content branding and consistency", "subtopics": ["Creator or brand kit", "Templates", "Recurring series and formats", "Consistent voice", "Signature elements (intro, colours, sound)"]}, {"title": "Copyright and responsible content use", "subtopics": ["What copyright protects", "Kenya's Copyright Act and the Kenya Copyright Board (KECOBO)", "Licences and permission", "Creative Commons and stock sites", "Fair dealing is limited - not a free pass", "Consent and model releases", "Children and vulnerable people", "Misinformation, defamation and hate speech", "Platform community guidelines", "Disclosing paid partnerships and AI-generated content"]}], "purpose": "Learners design graphics that look professional and tell a story, create a content brand kit and templates that keep their content consistent, and learn the copyright, consent and responsibility rules every creator must follow. They make a branded post, poster and carousel and start a credit and consent log.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Redesigning a Nakuru youth football tournament poster.", "The 'Otieno Shoots' brand kit with a 'Cheap Eats Friday' series.", "A Kibera recycling group carousel showing a dumpsite before and after a clean-up.", "A creator whose video was removed after using a film clip without permission."], "homework": ["Finish all three designs and fix the issues from the critique.", "Complete your credit and consent log for everything made so far, including your video.", "Choose the three to five best pieces for your portfolio."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and video results", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share the results of the video you published."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Graphic design fundamentals", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn the principles that make graphics look professional."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Visual storytelling", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Tell stories across carousels, posters and covers."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Content branding and consistency", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Build a brand kit and templates so your content is recognisable."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Copyright and responsible content use", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn the legal and ethical rules for using and creating content."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Design a branded post, poster and carousel", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Create your brand kit and three branded designs, and start your credit and consent log."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Peer critique and knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Give design feedback using the CRAP checklist and answer questions."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What do the letters in CRAP stand for?"}, {"question": "You downloaded a photo from Google Images. Can you use it in a client's poster?"}, {"question": "What is a model release and when do you need one?"}, {"question": "Name three things in a content brand kit."}, {"question": "What must you do when content is a paid partnership?"}], "objectives": ["Apply the design principles contrast, repetition, alignment and proximity, plus typography and colour basics", "Use grids and hierarchy to lay out a poster and a social post", "Tell a story across a carousel (hook slide, steps or before-and-after, payoff, call to action)", "Create a content brand kit (colours, fonts, logo or signature, templates, voice, recurring series)", "Explain copyright basics in Kenya, licences, Creative Commons, consent and model releases, and platform rules", "Keep a credit and consent log for all content"], "track_project": "A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Portfolio submissions for the programme use a restricted-access link shared only with named reviewers (for example a Google Drive folder set to \"Restricted\" and shared with the facilitator's email) or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN - never a public or \"anyone with the link\" link. A public portfolio is the learner's own optional choice, after consent checks. Privacy option: the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log.", "expected_outputs": ["Content brand kit with three templates", "Branded post, poster and carousel", "Credit and consent log (started)"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Design critique", "instructions": "Critique a partner's designs with the CRAP checklist and the 3-second test."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Licence check", "instructions": "Find one image on a free stock site and one Creative Commons image, and record the licence conditions for each."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Digital Marketing teaches a business brand identity to make a campaign consistent and persuasive. Content Creation teaches design and branding as a creative discipline and how a creator's own brand is built across content.", "AI Tools covers AI images and disclosure; here learners learn traditional design and the legal rules that apply to all content."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Content brand kit", "instructions": "Create a brand kit with colours, fonts, signature, style, voice, a recurring series and three templates."}, {"title": "Three branded designs", "instructions": "Design a post, a poster and a carousel that use your kit and tell a clear story."}, {"title": "Credit and consent log", "instructions": "Record the source and licence of everything you did not create, and the consent for everyone who appears."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"30fd4106-df82-4d41-b09a-e9bc66035166","session_id":"86656270-aaa1-472b-bda9-d1a65186e185","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Learners' pitches (slides or one-page visuals), pitch rubric, feedback cards or DIRA LEARN", "Timer and 30-day validation plan template"], "topics": [{"title": "Pitching and presenting business ideas", "subtopics": ["Delivering the pitch", "Answering questions", "Handling tough questions calmly"]}, {"title": "Pitching yourself", "subtopics": ["Entrepreneur self-pitch", "Using the project in job applications - skills employers value"]}, {"title": "Professional behaviour and workplace readiness", "subtopics": ["Showcase etiquette", "Receiving critical feedback", "Following up with guests and potential partners", "Pathways - starting, joining a business, employment, further learning"]}, {"title": "Next steps", "subtopics": ["30-day validation plan", "Support and networks for young entrepreneurs"]}], "purpose": "Learners pitch their early-stage business concept with evidence, answer questions about their numbers and assumptions, give and receive feedback, learn to present themselves as entrepreneurs and job candidates, and leave with a 30-day validation action plan.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A Kitui solar lamp rental pitch with a five-household test.", "A Nakuru laundry pick-up pitch with 12 pre-orders from hostel students.", "A learner who stopped a juice idea after testing and pitched a changed idea for office snack delivery."], "homework": ["Submit the pitch, business concept and 30-day validation plan in DIRA LEARN.", "Send thank-you messages to the customers and guests who helped."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Showcase set-up and expectations", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Pitches and questions - block 1", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The first six presenters in each room pitch and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Pitches and questions - block 2", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The next six presenters in each room pitch; recorded presenters answer their live questions."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Feedback round", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share feedback and learn from patterns across pitches."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Pitching yourself and workplace readiness", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Use your business project to pitch yourself as an entrepreneur or job candidate."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "30-day validation action plan", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write your next realistic validation step and a 30-day plan."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Celebration and close", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Celebrate and learn what happens next."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Pitch scored with the business pitch rubric."}, {"question": "What is your next validation step and how will you measure it?"}, {"question": "Describe your project using situation, action and result."}], "objectives": ["Deliver a 3-minute business pitch with evidence and a specific ask", "Answer questions about customers, numbers and assumptions", "Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric", "Pitch themselves as entrepreneurs and as employable young people using the project as proof", "Write a 30-day validation action plan"], "track_project": "A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed.", "expected_outputs": ["Delivered business pitch (final project) with rubric score and feedback", "Business concept summary (submitted in DIRA LEARN)", "30-day validation action plan", "Written feedback given to two peers"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Peer feedback", "instructions": "Give rubric-based written feedback to two assigned presenters."}, {"title": "Self-pitch practice", "instructions": "Practise your entrepreneur self-pitch in pairs."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Situation, action, result", "instructions": "Describe your business project in interview style using situation, action and result."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Every track ends with the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment; the Entrepreneurship & Business rubric weights evidence and numbers."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Business pitch", "instructions": "Deliver your 3-minute pitch with evidence and a specific ask, and answer questions for two minutes."}, {"title": "30-day validation plan", "instructions": "Complete the plan with the next validation step, weekly actions, measure, partner and risk."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"3623fd95-6896-4eaf-ab31-96004fc237d4","session_id":"9f499c8f-488f-4042-9513-80724e43c372","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Phone or computer with internet (pairs for learners without a device)", "Google Drive or phone file manager", "An AI assistant (any free one available in Kenya, such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot or Meta AI)", "LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook or WhatsApp (learners' own accounts, optional)", "Online safety scenario cards, digital safety checklist and core knowledge check"], "topics": [{"title": "Digital foundations for learning and work", "subtopics": ["Files and folders (cloud and phone)", "Naming files so you can find them", "Strong passwords and two-step verification", "Data-saving habits"]}, {"title": "Professional behaviour and workplace readiness online", "subtopics": ["Email and WhatsApp etiquette", "Video-call etiquette", "Response times and reliability", "Respectful disagreement", "Punctuality and keeping commitments"]}, {"title": "AI awareness", "subtopics": ["What AI tools are", "What they are good at", "Limitations (errors, made-up facts, bias, out-of-date information)", "Privacy - what never to paste into an AI tool", "Human judgement and disclosure"]}, {"title": "Communication and self-presentation", "subtopics": ["Clear messages (purpose, audience, request)", "Tone", "Listening and summarising", "Body language and voice on camera"]}, {"title": "Professional online presence", "subtopics": ["Profile photo, headline and bio", "Matching your personal brand statement", "What employers and clients check", "Privacy settings"]}, {"title": "Pitching yourself", "subtopics": ["The 30-second self-pitch (who, strength, proof, ask)", "Adapting it for an employer, client or funder"]}, {"title": "Online safety", "subtopics": ["Common scams in Kenya (fake jobs, M-Pesa reversal requests, fake prizes)", "Phishing links", "Sextortion and image-based abuse - what to do", "Reporting and support"]}, {"title": "Track confirmation", "subtopics": ["The four specialist tracks", "Matching the track to your goal"]}], "purpose": "Learners build the shared digital, communication and workplace-readiness foundation every specialist track depends on. They learn safe and professional online conduct, organise their learning files, see what AI tools can and cannot do, practise clear written and spoken communication, improve one professional online profile using their personal brand statement and deliver a 30-second self-pitch. They leave having confirmed their specialist track.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Fake job adverts on WhatsApp and Facebook that ask Kenyan job seekers for 'registration' or 'medical' fees before an interview.", "SMS messages claiming 'I have sent you money by mistake' that try to trick people into sending M-Pesa to strangers.", "A Nakuru graduate whose LinkedIn headline changed from 'Student' to 'Junior accountant | Helping small businesses keep clean records' and who started receiving recruiter messages.", "Using Meta AI in WhatsApp to draft a polite message to a landlord, then checking the facts before sending."], "homework": ["Finish your profile upgrade and ask your accountability partner for one improvement. If you prefer not to change a public profile, write the improved photo choice, headline and bio in a private document instead.", "Practise your 30-second pitch aloud three times; record it on your phone once and listen back.", "Make sure two-step verification is on for email and WhatsApp."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and digital confidence pulse check", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Recap session 1 and rate your digital confidence."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Digital foundations and professional conduct", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Organise your programme files, secure your accounts and learn the rules of professional online behaviour."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "AI awareness and prompt practice", "minutes": 20, "summary": "See what an AI assistant can and cannot do, write a prompt and check the output."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Clear communication practice", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write a professional message and email, and practise listening and summarising."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Professional online presence and self-pitch", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Improve one online profile so it matches your personal brand, then deliver a 30-second self-pitch."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Online safety scenarios", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Spot scams and privacy risks in real-looking scenarios and decide what to do."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Core module check and track confirmation", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Complete the short core knowledge check and confirm your specialist track."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Reflection, digital safety checklist and next steps", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Complete the digital safety checklist, reflect on the week and prepare for your track."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Name three things an AI assistant can help with and two of its limitations."}, {"question": "List three pieces of information you must never paste into an AI tool."}, {"question": "What does PAR stand for in a professional message?"}, {"question": "A WhatsApp job offer asks you to pay KES 1,500 before the interview. What do you do?"}, {"question": "Why should you turn on two-step verification on WhatsApp and email?"}, {"question": "Deliver your 30-second self-pitch. Did it include who, strength, proof and ask?"}, {"question": "What would an employer think of your online profile? Name one change you made."}, {"question": "Which track did you confirm and how does it connect to your goal?"}], "objectives": ["Apply five rules of professional online conduct (email, WhatsApp, video calls) and organise a programme folder", "Explain three things AI tools can help with, three limitations and why human checking is always required", "Write a clear prompt and improve the output using verification and their own judgement", "Write a professional message and an email with a clear subject, greeting, request and sign-off", "Improve one professional online profile (LinkedIn, Instagram bio, WhatsApp about or Facebook) so it matches their personal brand", "Deliver a 30-second self-pitch and give respectful feedback to a peer", "Recognise common online scams and privacy risks and know how to respond", "Confirm their specialist track choice with a reason"], "expected_outputs": ["Programme folder with the standard file names", "Short professional introduction message created with AI support, improved and checked by the learner, with a one-line AI disclosure", "Professional application email", "Improved online profile (or paper draft) matching the personal brand statement", "Delivered 30-second self-pitch", "Completed digital safety checklist and core knowledge check", "Confirmed specialist track"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Listening and summarising pairs", "instructions": "Explain your goal for 60 seconds; your partner summarises it in one sentence. Swap."}, {"title": "Self-pitch pairs", "instructions": "Deliver your 30-second pitch twice; your partner gives one 'keep' and one 'improve'."}, {"title": "Online safety scenarios", "instructions": "In groups, identify warning signs, actions and reporting routes for a scam or abuse scenario."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Prompt and improve", "instructions": "Write a prompt for your own introduction message, improve it twice and note what you corrected yourself."}, {"title": "Professional email", "instructions": "Write an application email with a clear subject line, greeting, two-sentence introduction, request and sign-off."}], "cross_track_notes": ["AI awareness here is the shared minimum for everyone. The AI Tools track goes much deeper (prompt engineering, research, automation, evaluation). Other tracks use AI only as an optional helper with the same disclosure rule.", "Professional online presence here is about the learner as a person. Digital Marketing session 3 applies the same ideas to a business's presence and reputation."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Profile upgrade", "instructions": "Improve one online profile (photo, headline, bio, contact, privacy) so it matches your personal brand statement. Private option: draft the improvements in a document without changing a public profile."}, {"title": "Digital safety checklist", "instructions": "Complete and tick every item on the checklist; ask for help with any you cannot do."}, {"title": "Track confirmation", "instructions": "Confirm your track in DIRA LEARN with one sentence linking it to your four-week goal."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"4a2f5bd2-2f48-4039-8c7e-73290b736fc5","session_id":"33a50c50-93d0-4483-8653-2833315c0964","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Learners' presentations (slides, one-page visual or phone screen sharing)", "Showcase rubric and feedback cards (or DIRA LEARN)", "Timer", "30-day action plan template and case study template"], "topics": [{"title": "Practical digital marketing campaign presentation", "subtopics": ["Structure of a campaign presentation", "Showing results honestly", "Explaining choices with evidence", "Answering questions"]}, {"title": "Pitching yourself and your work", "subtopics": ["Self-pitch plus project", "Turning the campaign into a portfolio case study", "Talking about your work in interviews and with clients"]}, {"title": "Professional behaviour and workplace readiness", "subtopics": ["Showcase etiquette (time, respect, listening)", "Receiving critical feedback", "Following up after an event", "Pathways - internships, freelance and online work, running your own business"]}, {"title": "Next steps", "subtopics": ["30-day action plan", "Ongoing learning", "Staying connected with DIRA alumni"]}], "purpose": "Learners demonstrate their practical digital marketing capability by presenting the campaign they built, explaining their choices and results, and answering questions. They learn to present their work as a portfolio piece to employers and clients, practise professional behaviour in a showcase setting and leave with a 30-day action plan.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A learner who presents a two-week campaign for a Ruiru bakery with 14 new orders and a cost per order of about KES 85.", "A learner who ran a zero-budget campaign for a Kibera youth recycling group and recruited 25 volunteers through WhatsApp groups.", "A learner who uses the campaign as proof in an application for a marketing assistant internship at a Nairobi SME."], "homework": ["Submit the final campaign, case study and 30-day plan in DIRA LEARN.", "Send a thank-you message and results summary to the business owner within two days."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Showcase set-up and expectations", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Campaign presentations and questions - block 1", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The first six presenters in each room present and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Campaign presentations and questions - block 2", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The next six presenters in each room present; recorded presenters answer their live questions."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Feedback round", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share feedback with peers and reflect on what you heard."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Pitching yourself and workplace readiness", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Turn your campaign into a portfolio piece and practise pitching yourself to an employer or client."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "30-day action plan", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write a 30-day plan for continuing the campaign or finding marketing work."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Celebration and close", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Celebrate the work and learn what happens next."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Presentation scored with the showcase rubric (objective and audience, content quality, channel and budget choices, measurement and results, presentation and answers)."}, {"question": "What would you change in your campaign if you ran it again, and why?"}, {"question": "Deliver your pitch using the campaign as proof."}], "objectives": ["Present a basic digital marketing campaign clearly in three minutes, explaining objective, audience, channels, content, budget and measures", "Report real results or test findings honestly and say what they would improve", "Answer questions about their choices with evidence", "Give and receive feedback professionally using the showcase rubric", "Pitch themselves and their campaign as a portfolio piece to an employer or client", "Write a 30-day action plan for continuing to market the business or to find marketing work"], "track_project": "A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission.", "expected_outputs": ["Presented digital marketing campaign (final project) with rubric score and feedback", "One-page campaign case study for the portfolio", "30-day action plan", "Written feedback given to two peers"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Peer feedback", "instructions": "Give rubric-based written feedback (one strength, one improvement) to the two presenters assigned to you."}, {"title": "Pitch practice", "instructions": "Practise your self-pitch with your campaign as proof, in pairs."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Campaign case study", "instructions": "Turn your campaign into a one-page case study with challenge, actions, results, learning and visuals."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Every track ends with a showcase using the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment, so all learners leave with a portfolio piece and a 30-day plan; the rubric differs by track."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Campaign presentation", "instructions": "Present your campaign in three minutes and answer questions for two minutes."}, {"title": "30-day action plan", "instructions": "Complete the plan with a goal, four weekly actions, a measure, an accountability partner and one risk."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"4abdd92d-8ea4-468b-a25c-edefb007e320","session_id":"6e1a25df-015a-49d9-9c8b-2940fca1a883","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Test card, lean plan and pitch templates, pitch rubric, timer", "Business model, finance template and interview notes from earlier sessions"], "topics": [{"title": "Testing business ideas", "subtopics": ["Minimum viable product", "Test types - pre-orders, pilots, sample sales, smoke tests", "Setting a success criterion before testing", "Reading results honestly", "Continue, change or stop"]}, {"title": "Business planning (completing the lean plan)", "subtopics": ["Lean one-page plan", "Legal basics - business name registration on eCitizen, KRA PIN, county permits (check current requirements)", "Funding options - savings, family, chamas, SACCOs, youth and government funds, grants, competitions", "Milestones"]}, {"title": "Pitching and presenting business ideas", "subtopics": ["Pitch structure", "Telling a story with evidence", "Numbers that matter", "The ask", "Delivery - voice, timing, confidence"]}], "purpose": "Learners design and run a small, cheap test of their riskiest assumption, decide what the evidence means, complete their lean business plan with legal and funding next steps, and build a 3-minute pitch backed by evidence. They review each other's pitches and revise their assumptions.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Pre-orders from 20 office workers for a juice delivery test.", "A Kisii uniform exchange running a one-day pilot before school opening.", "A Kitui solar lamp rental idea tested with five households for one week."], "homework": ["Run your test this week and record the result and your decision (continue, change or stop). Keep tests honest: do not take money for something you cannot deliver, and do not share customers' details.", "Add the result to your pitch and practise it three times within 3 minutes.", "Upload your business concept (opportunity statement, business model, finance template, marketing plan, lean plan) to DIRA LEARN.", "If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute pitch presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and sales script results", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share what happened when you used your sales script."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Testing business ideas", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how to test an idea quickly and cheaply before investing."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Design your test", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Design a test of your riskiest assumption with a clear success criterion."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Completing the lean business plan", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Add legal basics, funding options and milestones to your one-page plan."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Pitch structure", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn the structure of a short business pitch."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Build your pitch", "minutes": 25, "summary": "Build your 3-minute pitch with your evidence and numbers."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Peer pitch review", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Pitch to peers and use their feedback to improve."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What is a minimum viable product?"}, {"question": "Why set a success criterion before testing?"}, {"question": "Name three parts of a business pitch."}, {"question": "What should you do with pre-order money if you decide to stop?"}], "objectives": ["Design a test of their riskiest assumption (pre-orders, a pilot, a sample sale, a landing page or WhatsApp catalogue test)", "Define what result would support or reject the assumption before testing", "Interpret test evidence and decide to continue, change or stop", "Complete a lean one-page business plan including legal basics and funding options as next steps", "Build a 3-minute pitch (problem, solution, customer, evidence, model, money, team, ask)", "Give and use peer feedback to improve the pitch"], "track_project": "A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed.", "expected_outputs": ["Test card (and results if already tested)", "Completed lean one-page business plan", "Three-minute pitch with up to five slides or a one-page visual", "Peer review notes and revised assumptions"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Peer pitch review", "instructions": "Pitch to two peers who use the rubric to give two strengths and two improvements."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Success criterion", "instructions": "Write a success criterion with a number for your riskiest assumption before testing."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Every track builds its final project in session 7. The Entrepreneurship & Business project is judged on evidence, sound numbers and a clear pitch, not on how big the idea is."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Test card", "instructions": "Design a cheap, quick, ethical test of your riskiest assumption with a clear success criterion."}, {"title": "Lean one-page plan", "instructions": "Complete the plan including legal next steps, funding options and three milestones."}, {"title": "Three-minute pitch", "instructions": "Build your pitch with real evidence, key numbers and a specific ask."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"51ca77af-0f6c-4b4c-882d-7b5fc35d65b7","session_id":"caff2418-786e-415b-8812-bb2845d1a0ff","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Learners' solutions (live or recorded), showcase rubric, feedback cards or DIRA LEARN", "Timer, 30-day plan template and case study template", "Low-data showcase: a recorded demo can be a screen recording or a voice note with screenshots; play recordings from downloaded files, not streams."], "topics": [{"title": "Demonstrating an AI-assisted solution", "subtopics": ["Demo structure", "Live versus recorded demos", "Answering questions about accuracy and responsibility"]}, {"title": "Pitching yourself and your work", "subtopics": ["AI skills employers value (prompting, verification, workflow design)", "Turning the project into a case study", "Talking honestly about AI use"]}, {"title": "Professional behaviour and workplace readiness", "subtopics": ["Showcase etiquette", "Receiving critical feedback", "Following up with your user", "Pathways - jobs, freelancing, using AI in your own business"]}, {"title": "Next steps", "subtopics": ["30-day plan", "Keeping up with AI changes safely"]}], "purpose": "Learners demonstrate practical AI capability by showing a working AI-assisted solution, explaining how they checked it and used AI responsibly, and answering questions. They learn to present the project as portfolio evidence, practise professional behaviour and leave with a 30-day plan for using AI skills in study, work or business.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A learner demonstrating a revision quiz pack used by 15 Form Four students in Murang'a.", "A learner showing how a youth tournament registration workflow saved organisers hours of manual entry.", "A learner using the project as proof in an application for an administrative assistant role."], "homework": ["Submit the final solution, case study and 30-day plan in DIRA LEARN.", "Thank your user and share what you changed based on their feedback."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Showcase set-up and expectations", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Demonstrations and questions - block 1", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The first six presenters in each room demonstrate and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Demonstrations and questions - block 2", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The next six presenters in each room demonstrate; recorded presenters answer their live questions."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Feedback round and responsible AI reflection", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share feedback and reflect on what responsible AI use looked like in practice."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Pitching yourself and workplace readiness", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Turn your project into a portfolio case study and practise pitching your AI skills."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "30-day action plan", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write your 30-day plan for using AI skills."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Celebration and close", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Celebrate the AI solutions you built and learn what happens after the programme."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Demonstration scored with the AI showcase rubric."}, {"question": "Show one AI output that was wrong and explain how you caught and fixed it."}, {"question": "Deliver your pitch with your project as proof."}], "objectives": ["Demonstrate an AI-assisted solution clearly in three minutes", "Explain the human checks, privacy protections and disclosure in the solution", "Report user feedback and evaluation results honestly", "Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric", "Pitch themselves and the project as portfolio evidence", "Write a 30-day plan for using AI skills responsibly"], "track_project": "An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent.", "expected_outputs": ["Demonstrated AI-assisted solution (final project) with rubric score and feedback", "One-page AI project case study with disclosure", "30-day action plan", "Written feedback given to two peers"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Peer feedback", "instructions": "Give rubric-based written feedback to two assigned presenters."}, {"title": "Pitch practice", "instructions": "Practise your self-pitch with your project as proof."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "AI project case study", "instructions": "Write a one-page case study - problem, user, solution, checks, results, learning, disclosure."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Every track ends with the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment; the AI rubric weights evaluation and responsible use."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Demonstration", "instructions": "Demonstrate your solution in three minutes and answer questions for two minutes."}, {"title": "30-day plan", "instructions": "Complete the plan with goal, weekly actions, measure, accountability partner and risk."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"5a8405ce-8a89-4e8e-864e-da6ea0f6555b","session_id":"a123acb1-7333-44ae-92fb-d190c9ebd22c","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Interview guide template, opportunity statement template", "KNBS and county government websites (for secondary research)", "Phone for notes or recording (with permission)"], "topics": [{"title": "Identifying business opportunities", "subtopics": ["Problems as opportunities", "Trends (mobile money, online shopping, climate, urbanisation)", "Your skills, networks and resources", "Gaps in local markets", "Opportunity versus idea"]}, {"title": "Understanding customer problems", "subtopics": ["The job the customer is trying to do", "Pains and gains", "How customers solve the problem today", "Frequency and intensity of the problem", "Willingness to pay"]}, {"title": "Market research", "subtopics": ["Primary research - observation, interviews, short surveys", "Secondary research - KNBS data, county information, industry reports, news", "Asking good questions (past behaviour, not opinions about the future)", "Avoiding leading questions"]}, {"title": "Customer needs and target markets", "subtopics": ["Segments", "Target market", "Early adopters", "Rough market size (how many, how often, how much)", "Competition and alternatives"]}, {"title": "Assumptions versus evidence", "subtopics": ["Listing assumptions", "Riskiest assumptions first", "What counts as evidence"]}], "purpose": "Learners learn to find business opportunities in real problems around them, understand customers' problems deeply, do simple market research, and describe customer needs and target markets. They separate assumptions from evidence, prepare an interview guide and write a one-page opportunity statement.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A laundry pick-up service for university students in Nakuru.", "Dried mango snacks from mango waste in Machakos.", "A Kibera youth group collecting plastic for recycling companies.", "A second-hand school uniform exchange in Kisii before school opening."], "homework": ["Conduct at least five real customer interviews using your guide and record what you learned (quotes, numbers, surprises). Tell each person why you are asking and that they can decline; record answers without names unless they agree.", "Find one piece of secondary data about your market (with source and date)."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Welcome to Entrepreneurship & Business and track overview", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Meet your track group and see the six sessions and final business pitch."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Identifying business opportunities", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn where business opportunities come from and how to spot them."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Understanding customer problems", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Look at problems from the customer's point of view - the job, the pains and the gains."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Market research and target markets", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn simple ways to research a market and define your target customers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Demonstration - good and bad customer interviews", "minutes": 15, "summary": "See the difference between a leading interview and a good one that uncovers real behaviour."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Interview guide and practice interviews", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write your interview guide and practise interviewing in pairs."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Opportunity statement", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write a one-page opportunity statement for your idea."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What is the difference between an idea and an opportunity?"}, {"question": "Rewrite this leading question - 'Would you buy my cheap healthy juice?'"}, {"question": "What is the riskiest assumption in your idea?"}, {"question": "Name one primary and one secondary research method."}, {"question": "Who are early adopters?"}], "objectives": ["Identify business opportunities from problems, trends, skills and resources in their community", "Describe a customer problem in terms of the job the customer is trying to do, their pains and the gains they want", "Distinguish assumptions from evidence and list the riskiest assumptions in an idea", "Plan simple market research using primary (observation, interviews, surveys) and secondary (reports, data) sources", "Describe a target market and early adopters for an idea", "Conduct a practice customer interview using good, non-leading questions", "Write a one-page opportunity statement"], "track_project": "A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed.", "expected_outputs": ["Three community problems and an opportunity chosen with the filter", "Interview guide (eight to ten questions)", "One-page opportunity statement with top three assumptions"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Practice interviews", "instructions": "Interview a partner for five minutes each and get feedback on leading questions."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Opportunity filter", "instructions": "Run your three problems through the four opportunity filter questions and choose one."}, {"title": "Job, pains and gains", "instructions": "Map the job, pains, gains and current alternatives for your chosen problem."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Digital Marketing also studies customers, but to plan channels and messages for an existing offer. Here learners study customers to decide whether a business is worth starting at all."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Interview guide", "instructions": "Write eight to ten non-leading questions about past behaviour, current solutions, pains and spending."}, {"title": "Opportunity statement", "instructions": "Complete the one-page opportunity statement including your top three assumptions."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"62620224-d519-454b-a7f4-b7dfe4d2faaf","session_id":"f0b76394-6bbd-4eb6-83d8-40ce47578278","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Slides or flip chart with the programme map and the learning agreement", "Strengths map and goal card templates (printed or as a shared document)", "List of 20 values (printed or on a slide)", "Baseline self-assessment (DIRA LEARN or paper)", "Timer"], "topics": [{"title": "Programme orientation and learning agreement", "subtopics": ["How the four weeks work (core, then one specialist track)", "What a good learning space looks like", "Respect, consent and privacy in groups and online", "How to raise a concern, and the support contacts the programme has formally confirmed"]}, {"title": "Strengths, interests and values", "subtopics": ["Strengths versus skills", "Where strengths show up (school, family, church or mosque, chama, part-time work)", "Values as decision rules", "Spotting strengths in others"]}, {"title": "Self-leadership and confidence", "subtopics": ["Self-leadership - choosing your response", "The confidence loop (prepare, act, reflect)", "Fixed and growth mindset", "Self-talk that helps and self-talk that hurts"]}, {"title": "Personal branding (introduction)", "subtopics": ["What a personal brand is and is not", "Strengths plus values plus the help you offer", "Consistency between what you say and what you do", "First-draft personal brand statement"]}, {"title": "Confidence at work", "subtopics": ["Introducing yourself", "Asking for help without apologising", "Receiving feedback without defending", "Speaking up in a meeting or a WhatsApp group"]}, {"title": "Wellbeing and resilience", "subtopics": ["Sleep, movement, food and screen time", "Stress signals", "Small recovery habits", "Where to get support (friends, family, mentors, DIRA staff, professional help)"]}, {"title": "Goal setting and accountability", "subtopics": ["SMART goals", "First steps", "Accountability partners", "Tracking progress in DIRA LEARN"]}], "purpose": "Learners start the programme knowing what they are good at, what they care about and what they want from the next four weeks. They turn this self-knowledge into the first draft of a personal brand statement, practise confident behaviour they will need at work and in the programme, and learn where to get support. Everything later in DIRA LEARN (track projects, the showcase and the 30-day plan) builds on the goal card and personal brand statement made today.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A young woman in Kisumu who sells second-hand clothes online shows strengths in negotiation and photography that later become her personal brand as a reliable mitumba curator.", "A boda boda rider in Thika who organises his stage's welfare contributions shows leadership and trust, strengths an employer in logistics would value.", "A church youth leader in Eldoret who runs the WhatsApp group for 200 members shows communication and moderation skills.", "A learner preparing for an interview at a Nairobi supermarket chain uses the three confidence phrases on her first day as a merchandiser."], "homework": ["Show your personal brand statement to one person who knows you well and ask whether it sounds like you. Improve it.", "Take your first step towards your four-week goal and message your accountability partner by the end of the week.", "If you have social media or LinkedIn, look at your profile and note one thing that does not match your personal brand statement (you will fix it in session 2)."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Welcome and learning agreement", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Meet the facilitator, see how the four weeks work and agree how the group will learn together."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Icebreaker - two truths and a strength", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Introduce yourself with two true facts and one strength, and guess the strengths of others."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Values, confidence and self-leadership mini-workshop", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn the difference between strengths, skills and values, and how self-leadership and self-talk shape confidence."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Strengths map and personal brand statement", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Map your strengths, interests and values, and turn them into a first-draft personal brand statement."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Confidence at work - practice rounds", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Practise introducing yourself, asking for help and receiving feedback in short role-plays."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Wellbeing and resilience", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Recognise stress signals, choose small habits that help and know where to get support."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "SMART goal and accountability partner", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write one SMART four-week goal with a first step and agree check-ins with an accountability partner."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Reflection and exit ticket", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Complete the baseline self-assessment and an exit ticket so the facilitator can see where everyone is starting."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Name three of your strengths and give a real example of one of them."}, {"question": "What is the difference between a skill, a strength and a value?"}, {"question": "Read your personal brand statement. Who do you help, and how?"}, {"question": "Is your four-week goal SMART? Which part is the measurable part?"}, {"question": "Where can you go for support during the programme if you are struggling or feel unsafe?"}], "objectives": ["Describe at least three personal strengths, two interests and two values, with an example of each from real life", "Write a first-draft personal brand statement (two sentences) based on strengths, values and the help they can offer others", "Set one SMART four-week learning goal and name the first step they will take this week", "Practise three confident workplace behaviours (introducing yourself, asking for help, accepting feedback)", "Identify two wellbeing habits and one support route they can use during the programme", "Explain the programme expectations, the learning agreement and how to raise a safeguarding concern"], "expected_outputs": ["Completed strengths map (strengths, interests, values, help I can offer)", "First-draft personal brand statement (two sentences)", "Goal card with a SMART four-week goal, first step and two wellbeing habits", "Accountability partner agreed", "Baseline self-assessment and exit ticket submitted"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Two truths and a strength", "instructions": "In groups of four, share two true facts and one strength; others spot an extra strength in your story."}, {"title": "Confidence practice rounds", "instructions": "In pairs, practise introducing yourself, asking for help and receiving feedback in a work scenario."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Values cut-down", "instructions": "Choose five values from the list, cut to two, and write one moment when each guided a decision."}, {"title": "Rewrite your self-talk", "instructions": "Write one unhelpful sentence you say to yourself, then rewrite it as a helpful sentence that includes an action."}], "cross_track_notes": ["The personal brand statement made here is reused in session 2 (online profile) and in every track's session 8 showcase, where learners pitch themselves and their project."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Strengths map", "instructions": "Fill the four boxes (Strengths, Interests, Values, Help I can offer) with at least two items each and one real example."}, {"title": "Personal brand statement", "instructions": "Write a two-sentence statement using the formula. Keep it true, specific and about how you help others."}, {"title": "Goal card", "instructions": "Write one SMART goal, your first step this week and two wellbeing habits."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"63c49bfb-b32a-46f2-ab1b-ff619c333a89","session_id":"4d7fd967-9a3c-4e31-8fb2-e3a4af90cfcf","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Phones, paper storyboard template, strategy one-pager template", "Example videos (public, from Kenyan creators or prepared by the facilitator)", "Free and low-data planning: paper storyboards and strategy templates need no device or data; example videos downloaded in advance on programme Wi-Fi and shown on one screen."], "topics": [{"title": "Content strategy and planning", "subtopics": ["Purpose - why you create", "Audience - who it is for", "Niche - what you are known for", "Goals and measures", "Content pillars", "Consistency and a realistic rhythm", "Creator content versus brand content"]}, {"title": "Storytelling", "subtopics": ["The hook in the first 2 seconds", "Story arc - setup, tension, payoff", "Character, conflict and change", "Show, don't tell", "Emotion and authenticity", "Local stories and languages"]}, {"title": "Content formats for different platforms", "subtopics": ["Short vertical video (TikTok, Reels, Shorts) 9:16", "Feed photos and carousels (1:1 and 4:5)", "Stories and WhatsApp status", "Long-form video (YouTube 16:9)", "Audio and podcasts", "Text-led platforms (LinkedIn, X)"]}], "purpose": "Learners start the track as planners, not just posters. They define the purpose, audience and niche for their content, learn storytelling structures that hold attention, understand formats and specifications for each platform, and storyboard the first piece of their creative project.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A Kisumu creator reviewing affordable fish and ugali spots.", "A mama mboga's 4 am start at Marikiti market told as a 40-second story.", "A Nairobi tailor's 'fabric to fitting' transformation reel.", "A community clean-up in Nakuru told as a before-and-after carousel."], "homework": ["Turn your storyboard into a shot list (every shot you need to film).", "Visit or contact your brief (business or group) to plan filming and get consent. If you cannot get consent, or prefer not to work with a real brief, use the simulated brief from the facilitator and film objects, places or yourself.", "Watch five videos in your niche and note the hooks used."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Welcome to Content Creation and track overview", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Meet your track group and see the six sessions and the final portfolio."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Content strategy and planning", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how to define purpose, audience, niche, pillars and rhythm for content."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Storytelling", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn story structures that hold attention, with local examples."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Content formats for different platforms", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Match formats and specifications to platforms and audiences."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Demonstration - from idea to storyboard", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Watch a story idea turned into a six-frame storyboard."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Strategy one-pager, brief and storyboard", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Write your content strategy one-pager, choose your project brief and storyboard your first piece."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Peer pitch and knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Pitch your storyboard in 30 seconds and answer quick questions."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What is the difference between creator content and brand content?"}, {"question": "Write a hook for a video about cheap lunch spots in your town."}, {"question": "What does 'show, don't tell' mean?"}, {"question": "What is the aspect ratio and size for a TikTok or Reel?"}, {"question": "Name your niche and two content pillars."}], "objectives": ["Write a content strategy one-pager (purpose, audience, niche, goals, pillars, platforms, posting rhythm)", "Explain the difference between creator content and brand content", "Apply a story structure (hook, build, payoff, call to action) and the 'show, don't tell' principle", "Compare content formats and specifications for TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Facebook, WhatsApp status, LinkedIn and podcasts", "Choose a real brief for the creative project", "Storyboard a 30 to 60-second piece"], "track_project": "A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Portfolio submissions for the programme use a restricted-access link shared only with named reviewers (for example a Google Drive folder set to \"Restricted\" and shared with the facilitator's email) or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN - never a public or \"anyone with the link\" link. A public portfolio is the learner's own optional choice, after consent checks. Privacy option: the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log.", "expected_outputs": ["Content strategy one-pager", "Creative project brief", "Storyboard for a 30 to 60-second piece"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Storyboard pitch", "instructions": "Pitch your storyboard in 30 seconds; peers judge the hook and suggest one improvement."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Story anatomy", "instructions": "Watch two short videos and identify the hook, tension, payoff and call to action."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Digital Marketing plans content to reach a business goal through a campaign. Content Creation focuses on the craft - storytelling, production and the creator's portfolio - which can serve marketing but also education, causes and personal creator brands."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Content strategy one-pager", "instructions": "Write your purpose, audience, niche, goals, pillars, platforms and posting rhythm."}, {"title": "Storyboard", "instructions": "Storyboard a 30 to 60-second piece for your brief with six to eight frames."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"6a97b813-42ef-49f7-8110-0f9144a5fe0e","session_id":"9fb8a50c-010c-4757-b734-70dac518f759","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["One-page marketing plan, customer journey map and sales script templates", "Objection cards and negotiation cards"], "topics": [{"title": "Branding and marketing a small business", "subtopics": ["Simple brand - name, promise, look", "Positioning against alternatives", "Product, price, place and promotion", "Low-cost marketing - word of mouth, referrals, community, WhatsApp, social media", "One-page marketing plan"]}, {"title": "Customer service and customer experience", "subtopics": ["Customer journey - before, during, after purchase", "Moments of truth", "Service standards", "Handling complaints (listen, apologise, fix, follow up)", "Repeat customers and referrals"]}, {"title": "Sales and negotiation", "subtopics": ["Ethical selling", "Needs questions", "Features and benefits", "Handling objections (price, trust, timing)", "Closing and asking for the sale", "Negotiation preparation - goal, options, walk-away point", "Win-win with suppliers and customers"]}], "purpose": "Learners plan how to brand and market their small business on a small budget, design a customer experience that brings people back and handle complaints well, and practise ethical sales conversations and negotiation with customers and suppliers. They produce a one-page marketing plan, a customer journey map and a sales script.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A Wakulima market fruit supplier negotiation for a juice business.", "A Thika salon that sends a thank-you WhatsApp message and a loyalty stamp after each visit.", "A Kisii shoe repairer who turned a complaint into a loyal customer by fixing the shoe for free and following up."], "homework": ["Use your sales script with at least two real potential customers and note what happened.", "Choose the riskiest assumption from your business model to test in session 7."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and price reactions", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share how customers reacted to your price."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Branding and marketing a small business", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Plan a simple brand and low-cost marketing for your business."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "One-page marketing plan", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write the one-page marketing plan for your business."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Customer service and customer experience", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Design the experience that turns first-time buyers into repeat customers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Customer journey map and complaint role-play", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Map your customer journey and practise handling a complaint."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Sales and negotiation", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn ethical selling and basic negotiation with customers and suppliers."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Sales and negotiation role-play and sales script", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Practise a sales conversation and a supplier negotiation, then write your sales script."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Write your positioning sentence."}, {"question": "What are the four steps for handling a complaint?"}, {"question": "A customer says 'It is too expensive.' Give an ethical response."}, {"question": "What is a walk-away point in negotiation?"}, {"question": "Name two moments of truth in your customer journey."}], "objectives": ["Describe a simple brand for a small business (name, promise, look) and position it against alternatives", "Build a one-page marketing plan (target customer, message, channels, offer, budget, measures)", "Map the customer journey and design service standards for key moments", "Handle a customer complaint professionally", "Use ethical sales techniques - needs questions, benefits, handling objections, closing", "Negotiate with a supplier or customer using preparation, options and a walk-away point"], "track_project": "A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed.", "expected_outputs": ["One-page marketing plan", "Customer journey map with service standards", "Sales script"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Complaint role-play", "instructions": "In pairs, handle a complaint using Listen, Apologise, Fix, Follow up."}, {"title": "Sales and negotiation role-plays", "instructions": "In threes, practise a sales conversation and a supplier negotiation with an observer giving feedback."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Positioning sentence", "instructions": "Complete 'Customers choose us instead of [alternative] because [difference].'"}], "cross_track_notes": ["Digital Marketing teaches the online channels, copy and campaign measurement in depth. Here learners decide the overall marketing approach for the business, design the customer experience and practise face-to-face and phone selling and negotiation."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "One-page marketing plan", "instructions": "Write your target customer, key message, channels, first-customer offer, budget and measures."}, {"title": "Customer journey map", "instructions": "Map before, during and after purchase, mark two moments of truth and write a service standard for each."}, {"title": "Sales script", "instructions": "Write an opening, needs questions, benefits, objection answers and a closing question."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"6c405bf3-29aa-42c2-b516-21ae4286eed1","session_id":"c3dcef6d-faf9-47c9-8df7-cdb847f057e2","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Value proposition canvas and simplified business model canvas templates", "Challenge question cards", "Interview notes from homework"], "topics": [{"title": "Value propositions", "subtopics": ["Customer profile - jobs, pains, gains", "Value map - products and services, pain relievers, gain creators", "Fit between customer and offer", "Value proposition statement", "Differentiation from alternatives"]}, {"title": "Business models", "subtopics": ["Simplified business model canvas - customers, value proposition, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key activities, key resources, key partners, costs", "Business model types - product sales, services, subscriptions, commissions and marketplaces, rental, freemium", "Kenyan examples"]}, {"title": "Reaching and keeping customers in the business model", "subtopics": ["Channels - how customers find, buy and receive", "Digital marketing as a channel - social media, WhatsApp Business, content, advertising, search and measuring results (approved topics, business-model level)", "Customer relationships - self-service, personal service, community"]}, {"title": "Peer challenge", "subtopics": ["Testing the logic of a model", "Asking hard questions respectfully", "Revising the model"]}], "purpose": "Learners turn interview evidence into a clear value proposition and map how their business would create, deliver and capture value on a simplified business model canvas. They see at business-model level how the approved digital marketing topics fit the 'channels' and 'customer relationships' parts of the model, and test their model through a peer challenge. This session follows the session record approved by DIRA on 8 Nov 2026.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Fresh Fold laundry pick-up for students in Nakuru.", "A juice vendor (product sales), a phone repair shop (service), a tent and chair hire business (rental).", "A Kisumu fish seller who reaches office workers through WhatsApp status and delivers by boda boda."], "homework": ["Show your value proposition statement to two potential customers and record their reactions.", "List every cost you think your business will have (start-up and monthly) and find real prices for at least five of them."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and interview findings", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share what your customer interviews revealed."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Value propositions", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how to match your offer to customers' jobs, pains and gains."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Your value proposition", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Complete the value proposition canvas and statement for your idea using interview evidence."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Business models and the simplified canvas", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn the parts of a business model and common model types in Kenya."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Reaching and keeping customers - marketing in the business model", "minutes": 10, "summary": "See where the approved digital marketing topics fit in the channels and relationships boxes of your model."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Map your one-page business model", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Complete the simplified business model canvas for your idea."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Peer challenge", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Defend your business model against friendly but tough questions and improve it."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Write your value proposition statement."}, {"question": "Name five boxes of the simplified business model canvas."}, {"question": "Which business model type is a weekly laundry service, and why?"}, {"question": "What two channels will bring your first customers, and how will you measure them?"}, {"question": "Which box of your model is the biggest assumption?"}], "objectives": ["Summarise customer interview evidence into jobs, pains and gains", "Design a value proposition that matches products and services to customer pains and gains", "Write a one-sentence value proposition statement", "Explain the parts of a simplified business model canvas and common business model types in Kenya", "Choose how customers will be reached and kept (channels and relationships), including the role of digital marketing", "Map a one-page business model and defend it in a peer challenge"], "track_project": "A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed.", "expected_outputs": ["Value proposition canvas and one-sentence statement", "One-page business model (simplified canvas) with evidence and assumptions marked", "Peer challenge notes and a revised model"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Peer challenge", "instructions": "Present your model for two minutes and answer challenge questions from two peers; then swap roles."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Model type match", "instructions": "Decide which business model type fits your idea and consider one alternative model."}], "cross_track_notes": ["The digital marketing topics approved for this session are taught here only at business-model level (which channels, what message, how to measure). The Digital Marketing track teaches the same channels in depth; this avoids repeating the full content."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Value proposition canvas and statement", "instructions": "Complete the canvas with interview evidence and write your one-sentence statement."}, {"title": "One-page business model", "instructions": "Complete the simplified business model canvas and mark each box as evidence or assumption."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"6fc7160c-7b74-4c39-8f8a-25dc0c4213c6","session_id":"1f4e6189-e10c-41dc-9a45-1a32b4ea62ba","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Phones or computers with internet", "Google Search and Google Maps", "Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn and WhatsApp Business (for viewing; learners use their own accounts)", "Presence checklist, campaign brief template and channel map template", "Prepared screenshots for the demonstration (backup for poor connectivity)"], "topics": [{"title": "What digital marketing is", "subtopics": ["Marketing versus selling", "Digital versus traditional marketing (posters, radio, word of mouth)", "Why small businesses in Kenya market on phones", "What a campaign is"]}, {"title": "The customer journey", "subtopics": ["Awareness", "Consideration", "Conversion", "Loyalty and referral", "Where each channel helps"]}, {"title": "Digital marketing channels and strategies", "subtopics": ["Owned channels (profiles, WhatsApp Business, website, Google Business Profile)", "Earned channels (shares, reviews, word of mouth, media)", "Paid channels (boosted posts, ads, influencers)", "Marketplaces (Jumia, Kilimall, Facebook Marketplace)", "SMS and email", "Choosing channels by audience and budget"]}, {"title": "Digital footprint and online reputation", "subtopics": ["Active and passive footprint", "What employers and customers find when they search you", "Reviews and ratings", "Responding to complaints", "Protecting privacy and safety"]}, {"title": "Personal and business online presence", "subtopics": ["Name, photo or logo, bio and contact consistency", "Trust signals (reviews, real photos, location, opening hours, payment details)", "The difference between a personal and a business account"]}], "purpose": "Learners understand what digital marketing is, how customers move from first seeing a business to buying and returning, and which channels Kenyan small businesses use. They audit their own and a business's digital footprint, learn how reputation is built and repaired online, improve a profile, and choose the real business or cause they will build their campaign for.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Shiru's Bakes, a fictional Ruiru home bakery, used to illustrate the customer journey throughout the track.", "A mama mboga in Kawangware who takes orders by WhatsApp and announces fresh deliveries on WhatsApp status.", "A kinyozi in Kahawa West that gained new customers after listing on Google Maps with photos and opening hours.", "A Gikomba mitumba trader who sells on Facebook Marketplace and TikTok live.", "A youth recycling group in Kibera using Facebook and X to recruit volunteers and find buyers for recycled products."], "homework": ["Talk to the business owner (or yourself, if it is your idea) and confirm the brief - what they sell, who buys, and what they want to improve. Ask for written permission (a WhatsApp message is enough) to use the business's name, logo, photos and prices. If you cannot get permission, or prefer not to work in public, use the simulated brief from the facilitator.", "Make the profile improvements you wrote in class.", "Ask three real customers of the business how they first heard about it. Bring their answers to session 4. Tell them why you are asking, and note answers without names."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Welcome to Digital Marketing and track overview", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Meet your track group, see the six sessions and the final campaign you will build."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "What digital marketing is, the customer journey and channels", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how customers move from noticing a business to buying again, and which channels help at each stage."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Digital footprint and online reputation", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Understand what your online trail says about you or a business, and how reputation is protected and repaired."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Demonstration - footprint audit and review reply", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Watch a live audit of a business's online presence and a professional reply to a bad review."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Presence audit and profile improvement", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Audit your own footprint and a business profile, then improve one profile."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Choose your campaign brief and draw a channel map", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Pick the real business or cause for your campaign and map which channels its customers use."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Answer short questions on the customer journey, channels and reputation."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise the session and confirm your homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Name the four stages of the customer journey and one channel that helps at each."}, {"question": "What is the difference between owned, earned and paid channels? Give an example of each."}, {"question": "What is a passive digital footprint?"}, {"question": "A customer posts an angry review. Write the first sentence of your reply."}, {"question": "Why should a business use a business phone number on its profiles?"}], "objectives": ["Explain digital marketing and the customer journey (awareness, consideration, conversion, loyalty) using a local business example", "Compare owned, earned and paid channels and name at least five digital channels used by Kenyan businesses", "Audit a digital footprint (personal and business) and identify three risks and three improvements", "Respond professionally to a negative online review", "Improve one personal or business profile for clarity, trust and contact", "Choose a campaign brief and draw a first channel map for it"], "track_project": "A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission.", "expected_outputs": ["Personal digital footprint audit (kept private) with privacy settings fixed", "Improved personal or business profile (bio, image, contact, trust signals)", "One-paragraph campaign brief", "Channel map with two main channels and one support channel"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Channel map challenge", "instructions": "Present your brief to two peers, draw the channel map, choose two main channels and one support channel and defend your choice."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Customer journey mapping", "instructions": "Map one business you know through awareness, consideration, conversion and loyalty, naming the channel at each step."}, {"title": "Review reply", "instructions": "Write an ACE reply to a fictional complaint about a late delivery or a wrong order."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Core session 2 covered the learner's personal profile. Here the focus is a business's presence and reputation and the strategy behind channel choice.", "Content Creation also covers platform formats, but from the creator's craft angle; Digital Marketing chooses channels and content to reach a business goal."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Personal footprint audit", "instructions": "Search your name and usernames, list what you find, fix privacy settings and remove anything you would not want an employer to see."}, {"title": "Business profile improvement", "instructions": "Audit one business profile with the presence checklist and write the improved bio, contact line and profile image choice."}, {"title": "Campaign brief", "instructions": "Write a one-paragraph brief for the business or cause you will market in this track. Choose a real business or cause (with the owner's agreement) or the simulated brief from the facilitator."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"7753d94e-5ff2-44c3-8d3a-670d42f9c4d1","session_id":"b46fd49d-d091-4eeb-aab7-10373005b48a","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["AI tools from the track, Google Forms and Sheets, WhatsApp Business (as needed)", "Project design canvas, AI quality checklist and demo template", "User feedback form", "No paid subscription needed: a solution can be a prompt set with instructions in a free document, a Google Form, or a WhatsApp message flow; build in pairs on a shared device if needed."], "topics": [{"title": "Designing an AI-assisted solution", "subtopics": ["Problem and user", "What AI does and what humans do", "Tools and prompts", "Success test - how you will know it works"]}, {"title": "Building and documenting", "subtopics": ["Prompt set with placeholders", "Example inputs and outputs", "Instructions for the user"]}, {"title": "Testing and evaluating AI outputs", "subtopics": ["AI quality checklist - accuracy, usefulness, safety, privacy, bias, disclosure", "User testing", "Revising based on evidence"]}, {"title": "Preparing a demonstration", "subtopics": ["Demo structure", "Showing before and after", "Explaining responsible choices"]}], "purpose": "Learners combine everything from the track into one practical AI-assisted solution for a real person, business or group. They build it, evaluate it for accuracy, usefulness, safety, privacy and bias, test it with a real user, revise it and prepare to demonstrate it in the showcase.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A customer reply kit for a Kitengela hardware shop.", "A KCSE revision pack with AI-generated quizzes checked against the syllabus by a teacher.", "An AI-assisted information pack for a Kisumu community health volunteer group, checked by a health worker before use.", "A registration and reminder workflow for a youth sports tournament in Nyeri."], "homework": ["Complete the real user test and record the feedback.", "Finish your demonstration and practise it twice within three minutes.", "Upload your solution files, prompt set, checklist and user feedback to DIRA LEARN.", "If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute solution presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Project check-in", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Confirm your project brief and success test."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Designing your solution", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Plan the solution using the project design canvas."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Build", "minutes": 40, "summary": "Build your AI-assisted solution and document the prompts and outputs."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Evaluate and user-test", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Score your solution with the AI quality checklist and test it with a real user or a peer."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Revise and prepare the demonstration", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Fix the main problems and prepare a three-minute demo."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Showcase briefing and homework", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn how the showcase works and what to submit."}], "assessment": [{"question": "AI quality checklist completed for the solution."}, {"question": "What is your success test, and did the solution pass?"}, {"question": "Where is the human check in your solution, and why there?"}], "objectives": ["Define the problem, user, AI role and success test for the project", "Build a working AI-assisted solution using a documented prompt set and tools", "Evaluate the solution with the AI quality checklist and fix problems", "Test the solution with a real user and record their feedback", "Prepare a three-minute demonstration with an AI-use disclosure"], "track_project": "An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent.", "expected_outputs": ["Completed project design canvas", "Working AI-assisted solution with documented prompt set and user instructions", "AI quality checklist and user test record", "Draft three-minute demonstration"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Demo run-through", "instructions": "Run a 1-minute version of your demo for a partner and get one improvement."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Project design canvas", "instructions": "Complete the canvas - user, problem, AI role, human role, tools, prompts, data protection, risks and checks, success test."}], "cross_track_notes": ["All tracks build their final project in session 7. The AI Tools project is judged on usefulness, responsible AI use and evaluation, not on design polish."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Build the solution", "instructions": "Build and document the solution - prompt set, example inputs and outputs, user instructions."}, {"title": "Quality checklist", "instructions": "Score the solution on each checklist item and fix anything scoring 1."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"7a1b3c68-1ecc-4da3-8f84-dc97f71ddd9b","session_id":"e3e3c255-93c2-456d-98cb-29570193e4eb","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Campaign template, budget sheet and tracking sheet (Google Sheets or paper)", "Screenshots of Meta boost and Ads Manager, and page insights", "Calculator", "Campaign checklist and showcase rubric"], "topics": [{"title": "Introduction to online advertising", "subtopics": ["Organic versus paid reach", "Boosted posts versus Ads Manager", "Objectives (awareness, traffic, engagement, messages, sales)", "Audience targeting (location, age, interests, custom audiences)", "Ad creative and copy", "Ad policies and honest advertising", "Overview of Google Search ads", "Micro-influencers and paid partnerships (disclosure)"]}, {"title": "Campaign planning and budgeting", "subtopics": ["SMART campaign objectives", "Campaign timeline", "Budget lines in KES (content, data, ads, printing, delivery, incentives)", "Daily versus lifetime budget", "Estimating cost per result", "Starting small and testing"]}, {"title": "Marketing analytics and performance measurement", "subtopics": ["Key performance indicators linked to objectives", "Reach, impressions, engagement rate", "Clicks and messages", "Conversions and cost per conversion", "Return on ad spend", "Platform insights", "Tracking offline sales (codes, asking 'how did you hear about us?')", "Simple tracking sheet"]}, {"title": "Practical campaign development", "subtopics": ["Putting the parts together", "Checking consistency", "Peer testing", "Revising"]}], "purpose": "Learners understand how paid online advertising works and when a small business should use it, plan a realistic KES campaign budget, choose key performance indicators and how to track them, and assemble every part of their campaign into one presentable plan that they test with peers.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Shiru's Bakes KES 3,000 campaign with KES 1,500 ads, aiming for 20 orders in two weeks.", "A Kisumu fish seller using the code 'SAMAKI5' on WhatsApp status to see which customers came from the campaign.", "A Nairobi youth tech hub boosting an event post to people aged 18 to 30 within 15 km.", "A community clean-up group in Kitale running a zero-budget campaign through church and school WhatsApp groups (with admins' permission)."], "homework": ["Finish the campaign and run at least one part of it for real (posts, status or broadcast with consent) and record the results in the tracking sheet. Private option: run it as a private test (peers, or a WhatsApp group that agreed) or present it as a simulation with clearly labelled estimates.", "Prepare a five-slide presentation or one-page visual for the showcase.", "Practise your three-minute presentation twice, including your self-pitch introduction.", "If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute campaign presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and results from your first post", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share the reach and engagement from the post you published."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Introduction to online advertising", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how paid ads work on social media and search, and when a small business should use them."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Campaign planning and budgeting in KES", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Plan a campaign timeline and a realistic budget with an estimated cost per result."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Marketing analytics and measuring results", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Choose KPIs for your objective and learn how to read and track the numbers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Campaign build", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Assemble your complete campaign plan using the campaign template."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Peer testing and revision", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Test your campaign with peers using the checklist and improve it."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Showcase preparation and homework", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn how session 8 works and what to prepare."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What is the difference between reach and impressions?"}, {"question": "You spent KES 1,000 on ads, got 25 messages and 5 orders. What is the cost per order?"}, {"question": "Which KPI fits the objective 'get 20 WhatsApp orders'?"}, {"question": "Why should a business fix its offer and WhatsApp replies before paying for ads?"}, {"question": "How can you measure sales that happen offline or on WhatsApp?"}], "objectives": ["Explain the difference between boosting a post and creating an ad in Meta Ads Manager, and give an overview of Google Ads", "Choose an ad objective, audience, creative and budget for a small campaign", "Build a campaign budget in KES covering content, data, ads and other costs, with a cost-per-result estimate", "Explain reach, impressions, engagement rate, clicks, messages, conversions, cost per result and return on ad spend", "Read basic insights on Facebook, Instagram or WhatsApp Business and track offline results with simple codes", "Assemble a complete campaign plan with a SMART objective, KPIs and a timeline", "Improve the campaign based on peer testing"], "track_project": "A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission.", "expected_outputs": ["Complete campaign plan (all twelve sections)", "Campaign budget in KES with cost-per-result estimate (or zero-budget time plan)", "KPI list with targets and a tracking sheet", "Peer-test feedback and a revision list"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Campaign peer test", "instructions": "Present your campaign in three minutes; peers use the checklist to give two strengths and two improvements."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Cost-per-result calculation", "instructions": "Given ad spend, conversations and orders, calculate cost per conversation and cost per order and decide if the ad was worth it."}, {"title": "Match KPIs to objectives", "instructions": "For three objectives (awareness, messages, sales), choose the right two KPIs each."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Entrepreneurship & Business covers business budgeting and cash flow for the whole business; Digital Marketing budgets only the campaign and calculates its cost per result.", "AI Tools may be used to draft campaign copy or analyse results, with disclosure and human checking."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Campaign budget", "instructions": "Build your campaign budget in KES with at least five lines and an estimated cost per result (or a zero-budget time plan)."}, {"title": "Complete campaign plan", "instructions": "Fill all twelve sections of the campaign template."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"8645ada2-0253-4e68-8703-54ad6b2daf5b","session_id":"ad880c63-14d6-408b-8b6a-646e961c1bf6","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Finance template (Google Sheets or paper) and calculator", "Cost lists and price research from homework", "Lean one-page business plan template"], "topics": [{"title": "Basic financial literacy for entrepreneurs", "subtopics": ["Separating business and personal money", "Simple record keeping (sales book, expense book, M-Pesa statements)", "Paying yourself a fixed amount", "Savings and emergency funds", "Credit - digital loans, the Hustler Fund, chamas, SACCOs, banks, interest and the risk of debt", "Tax basics - KRA PIN and why records matter"]}, {"title": "Pricing and costing", "subtopics": ["Start-up versus operating costs", "Fixed versus variable costs", "Variable cost per unit and fully allocated unit cost", "Cost-plus pricing", "Competitor-based pricing", "Value-based pricing", "Discounts and their effect on profit"]}, {"title": "Revenue and expenses", "subtopics": ["Revenue (sales)", "Cost of goods sold", "Contribution per unit and contribution margin", "Gross profit and net profit", "Simple monthly profit statement", "Break-even"]}, {"title": "Cash-flow fundamentals", "subtopics": ["Profit is not cash", "Timing of money in and out", "Credit sales and stock", "One-month cash-flow estimate", "Keeping a cash buffer"]}, {"title": "Business planning", "subtopics": ["Lean one-page business plan", "What to include", "When a full business plan is needed (loans, investors, competitions)"]}], "purpose": "Learners build basic financial literacy for running a small business - separating business and personal money, keeping records, understanding credit - and learn to cost a product, set a price, calculate profit and break-even, and plan cash flow. They produce a start-up budget, a one-month cash-flow estimate and a lean one-page business plan outline.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A juice business with a KES 50 variable cost per bottle, a fully allocated cost of about KES 63 at 300 bottles a month, and a KES 80 price.", "A Kericho mama mboga who separated her M-Pesa line and found she was losing money on transport.", "A Nairobi phone repair technician who ran out of cash because customers collected phones on credit.", "Comparing a digital loan with a chama loan for buying stock."], "homework": ["Replace at least three guesses in your finance template with researched numbers.", "Show your price to three potential customers and record their reactions."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and cost hunt results", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share the real costs you found."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Basic financial literacy for entrepreneurs", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn the money habits that keep a small business alive."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Costing", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Classify your costs and calculate the cost of one unit."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Pricing and break-even", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Set a price using three methods, then calculate contribution per unit and break-even."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Revenue, expenses and cash flow", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Understand profit and cash flow and why they differ."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Start-up budget and one-month cash flow", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Build your start-up budget, unit cost, price, break-even and one-month cash-flow estimate."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Lean one-page business plan", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn the outline of a lean business plan and when a full plan is needed."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Classify rent and packaging as fixed or variable costs."}, {"question": "Your variable cost per unit is KES 120, your fully allocated unit cost at expected sales is KES 150, and competitors charge KES 180 to 220. Suggest a price and explain."}, {"question": "Fixed costs are KES 6,000 a month and contribution per unit is KES 40. What is break-even?"}, {"question": "Variable cost per unit is KES 50, fixed costs are KES 4,000 a month and you expect to sell 200 units at KES 90. Work out the contribution per unit and the fully allocated unit cost."}, {"question": "Why can a profitable business run out of cash?"}, {"question": "Name two questions to ask before taking a loan."}], "objectives": ["Explain why business and personal money must be separated and how to keep simple records (including M-Pesa statements)", "Describe the risks and costs of borrowing (digital loans, Hustler Fund, chamas, SACCOs, banks)", "Classify start-up and operating costs, and fixed and variable costs", "Calculate variable cost per unit, contribution per unit, gross profit and fully allocated unit cost, and set a price using cost-plus, competitor and value methods", "Calculate break-even in units using contribution per unit", "Explain the difference between profit and cash flow, and build a one-month cash-flow estimate", "Outline a lean one-page business plan"], "track_project": "A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed.", "expected_outputs": ["Start-up budget with researched prices", "Variable and fully allocated unit cost, price, contribution per unit, gross profit and break-even calculation", "One-month cash-flow estimate", "Lean one-page business plan outline (started)"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Number check", "instructions": "Swap templates with a partner and check one calculation each."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Cost sorting", "instructions": "Sort your costs into start-up or operating and fixed or variable."}, {"title": "Discount effect", "instructions": "Calculate how a 10% discount changes your contribution per unit and your break-even."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Digital Marketing budgets only a campaign and its cost per result. Entrepreneurship & Business budgets the whole business - start-up costs, pricing, profit and cash flow."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Finance template", "instructions": "Complete the start-up budget, variable and fully allocated unit cost, price, contribution per unit, break-even, monthly gross and net profit and one-month cash flow, highlighting guesses."}, {"title": "One-page plan outline", "instructions": "Start the lean one-page business plan with what you have from sessions 3 to 5."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"93fcd723-1f5e-4704-8862-35bba9c2b24c","session_id":"6c899251-1167-4d98-b406-bd7762ae75a7","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Free AI tools (free tiers; limits and sign-in rules change, so check them in the week of the session): ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Microsoft Copilot and Claude for prompting practice; Perplexity for answers that show sources. Most need a free account for full use.", "No-account alternative: Duck.ai (DuckDuckGo's free AI chat at duck.ai) works in any phone or computer browser without an account, email or phone number, with anonymised chats and a daily limit. ChatGPT and Perplexity can also be used logged out with tighter limits. If none works on the venue network, learners follow the facilitator's demo on one shared screen and write their prompts on paper.", "Search engine and official websites (eCitizen, KRA, Kenya Law, KNBS)", "Prompt library template, evaluation checklist and research brief template", "Low-data option: text prompts only; keep the prompt library as a phone note or on paper; use the text pages of official sites rather than videos."], "topics": [{"title": "Prompt engineering", "subtopics": ["The RTCF framework - Role, Task, Context, Format", "Constraints (length, tone, audience, language)", "Giving examples (few-shot prompting)", "Prompt patterns - step by step, ask me questions first, critique and improve, compare options", "Iterating and follow-up prompts", "Building a reusable prompt library"]}, {"title": "AI for research and learning", "subtopics": ["AI as a tutor - explain, simplify, quiz me", "Summarising long texts you are allowed to use", "Planning research questions", "AI research tools that show sources", "Limits - invented references and outdated facts"]}, {"title": "Fact-checking and evaluating AI outputs", "subtopics": ["Stop - do not share yet", "Lateral reading (check what other sources say)", "Official and reliable sources in Kenya (government, KNBS, Kenya Law, recognised media, academic sources)", "Checking numbers, dates, names, quotes and references", "Accuracy, relevance, completeness and bias checklist"]}], "purpose": "Learners move from casual questions to deliberate prompting. They learn a prompt framework and advanced patterns, use AI as a study and research partner, and apply a fact-checking routine to AI outputs. They produce the first version of their personal prompt library and a verified research brief.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A Mombasa hospitality graduate using RTCF to get CV bullet points for hotel jobs.", "Checking business-name registration steps against the official eCitizen Business Registration Service pages instead of trusting an AI answer.", "An AI tool inventing academic references on youth unemployment in Kenya that do not exist.", "A KCSE leaver using AI to quiz themselves on a topic before a college entry test."], "homework": ["Add two more prompts to your prompt library and test them on real tasks.", "Choose your final project brief and write one paragraph - who it helps, what problem it solves, and how AI will help."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and task log review", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Review what needed checking in your AI tasks."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Prompt engineering - framework and patterns", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn the RTCF framework and prompt patterns that produce better outputs."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Prompt lab", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Rewrite weak prompts into strong ones, compare outputs and start your prompt library."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "AI for research and learning", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn to use AI as a tutor and research planner without trusting it blindly."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Demonstration and practice - fact-checking AI outputs", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Watch the fact-checking routine applied to an AI answer, then use it yourself."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Verified research brief", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Use AI to plan and draft a short research brief, then verify every key claim."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Answer questions on prompting, research and fact-checking."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What do the letters RTCF stand for?"}, {"question": "Improve this prompt - 'make me a business plan'."}, {"question": "What is lateral reading?"}, {"question": "An AI gives you a reference to a report. How do you check it?"}, {"question": "Name four things to check in an AI output."}], "objectives": ["Use the RTCF prompt framework (Role, Task, Context, Format) plus constraints and examples", "Apply at least four prompt patterns (step by step, ask me questions first, critique and improve, give examples)", "Iterate a prompt and explain why the output improved", "Use AI to learn a topic (explain, quiz, summarise, compare) and to plan research", "Fact-check AI outputs using lateral reading and official or reliable sources", "Produce a short research brief in which every key claim has a verified source"], "track_project": "An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent.", "expected_outputs": ["Prompt library v1 (three tested prompts)", "Fact-check record for one AI answer", "Verified research brief with sources and disclosure"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Prompt swap", "instructions": "Test a partner's prompt and suggest one improvement."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Prompt rewrite", "instructions": "Rewrite four weak prompts with RTCF for a specific Kenyan situation and compare outputs."}, {"title": "Claim check", "instructions": "Mark each claim in an AI answer as confirmed, wrong or not found, with the source used."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Other tracks use AI only as an optional helper. The AI Tools track teaches prompting and verification as core skills that learners can then apply to marketing, content or business work."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Prompt library v1", "instructions": "Save your three best prompts with their purpose, the prompt text and tips for using them."}, {"title": "Verified research brief", "instructions": "Draft a 200-word brief with AI help, verify at least five claims and list sources beside them."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"af294d40-bb58-4b91-9b8b-9884a8f1d683","session_id":"413e6de4-6a34-47bc-b4c5-8d826bd7760e","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["AI chat assistant; a free AI image tool available in Kenya", "Google Slides, PowerPoint or Canva", "Writing workflow card and image prompt template", "Free or low-cost alternatives: free tiers of image tools (daily limits apply) - or, where image generation is not possible, write and improve the image prompt and sketch the result on paper; Google Slides or Canva free, or the phone's built-in office app offline. At most two generated images per learner on mobile data."], "topics": [{"title": "AI-assisted writing", "subtopics": ["Human first and last workflow", "Drafting, editing, shortening and changing tone", "Keeping your own voice", "Translation and checking Kiswahili or other languages", "CVs, cover letters and emails - truthfully", "Academic integrity and plagiarism"]}, {"title": "AI-generated images and creative work", "subtopics": ["How image prompts work - subject, setting, style, lighting, composition", "Editing and variations", "Common image problems (hands, text, odd details)", "Bias in generated people and places", "Deepfakes, consent and real people", "Copyright uncertainty and labelling AI images"]}, {"title": "AI for presentations", "subtopics": ["Outlining a presentation with AI", "One idea per slide", "AI features in slide and design tools", "Checking facts, numbers and images", "Speaker notes and rehearsal"]}], "purpose": "Learners apply prompting to three common outputs - writing, images and presentations - with a 'human first and last' workflow. They produce an AI-assisted piece of writing in their own voice, an AI-assisted image with an honest label, and a short presentation, all checked and disclosed.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A hospitality graduate improving a cover letter for a hotel in Diani without adding false experience.", "A youth group in Machakos preparing a talk on safe AI use for job seekers.", "AI images that wrongly show Kenyan markets or put the wrong currency on price tags.", "A Kakamega farmers' group using an AI illustration for a training poster, labelled as AI-generated."], "homework": ["Improve your presentation using the gallery feedback.", "Write the project plan for session 7 - problem, user, AI tools you will use, human checks, and how you will test it with a real user."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and project brief check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share your final project idea in one sentence."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "AI-assisted writing", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn a responsible workflow for writing with AI while keeping your own voice."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Write with AI in your own voice", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Produce a short piece of real writing with AI help and edit it into your own voice."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "AI images and creative work", "minutes": 20, "summary": "See how image prompts work, what goes wrong, and how to use AI images responsibly."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Create an AI-assisted image", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write an image prompt, improve it twice and label the final image."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "AI for presentations - demonstration and build", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Outline and build a five-slide presentation with AI support and check it."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Gallery review and knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Review each other's work and answer quick questions."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What are the five steps of the human-first-and-last workflow?"}, {"question": "Why must you never let AI add experience to your CV?"}, {"question": "Name the five parts of a good image prompt."}, {"question": "When should a business use a real photo instead of an AI image?"}, {"question": "What should the last slide of an AI-assisted presentation include?"}], "objectives": ["Use the human-first-and-last workflow (plan, prompt, edit, check, disclose) for writing", "Use AI to draft, edit, shorten, change tone and translate, while keeping their own voice and facts", "Write image prompts with subject, setting, style, lighting and composition, and evaluate results", "Explain the risks of AI images (deepfakes, consent, bias, copyright uncertainty) and label AI images honestly", "Plan and build a five-slide presentation with AI support and check it for accuracy and clarity", "Disclose AI assistance appropriately"], "track_project": "An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent.", "expected_outputs": ["AI-assisted writing piece (draft and edited version)", "Labelled AI-assisted image with prompt notes", "Five-slide presentation with speaker notes and disclosure"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Voice check", "instructions": "A partner reads your final writing and says whether it sounds like you."}, {"title": "Gallery review", "instructions": "Comment on two classmates' images and slides with one strength and one fix."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Remove the robot", "instructions": "Take a generic AI paragraph and rewrite it with your own voice, a personal detail and a local example."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Content Creation teaches photography, video and design by hand; AI Tools teaches when and how AI can assist creative work and its limits. Digital Marketing uses captions and graphics for a campaign; AI Tools focuses on the AI workflow and checking."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "AI-assisted writing piece", "instructions": "Produce a real piece of writing using the five-step workflow, showing the AI draft and your edited version."}, {"title": "AI-assisted image", "instructions": "Create and label an image for your project using a five-part prompt improved twice. Do not create images of real, identifiable people without their consent."}, {"title": "Five-slide presentation", "instructions": "Build a five-slide presentation with speaker notes, checked facts and a disclosure line."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"b20f0fd2-e394-4d47-867e-a189eff9ea5e","session_id":"3eddf42c-25c2-44f3-8bba-2f8035458587","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Caption template, scenario cards, Google Business Profile template", "Google Search (autocomplete, People also ask) and Google Maps", "WhatsApp Business (from session 4)"], "topics": [{"title": "Copywriting and persuasive messaging", "subtopics": ["Hooks and first lines", "Features versus benefits", "AIDA (Attention, Interest, Desire, Action)", "Problem-Agitate-Solution", "Calls to action", "Tone and language (English, Kiswahili, Sheng)", "Honesty and avoiding misleading claims"]}, {"title": "Customer engagement and conversion", "subtopics": ["Reply speed and tone", "From comment to DM to order", "Social proof (reviews, testimonials with consent, numbers)", "Offers and urgency without pressure", "Reducing friction (prices, delivery, M-Pesa till or paybill)", "Follow-up and repeat customers"]}, {"title": "Search engine optimisation fundamentals", "subtopics": ["How search works (crawling, indexing, ranking)", "Keywords and search intent", "Local SEO and 'near me' searches", "Google Business Profile (category, hours, photos, reviews, posts)", "Simple on-page SEO for a website or blog", "Consistent name, address and phone across the web"]}], "purpose": "Learners write captions and messages that make people act, handle enquiries so they become sales, and make the business easier to find on Google Search and Maps. They leave with finished captions, a customer conversation script and a local search plan.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Shiru's Bakes captions in AIDA and PAS.", "A Thika boda boda delivery business that ranks for 'parcel delivery Thika' after completing its Google Business Profile and collecting reviews.", "A kinyozi in Kahawa West replying to every Google review with the customer's first name and a thank-you.", "An Eldoret shoe seller who lost sales by replying 'inbox' to price questions, then gained orders by posting prices in the catalogue."], "homework": ["Publish one of your posts with its caption on the business's account (with the owner's permission) or your own account, and note the reach and engagement after 48 hours. Private option: share it with a small group of people who agreed to take part, or test it with three peers, and record their reactions instead of public reach.", "Bring all campaign parts to session 7."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and caption hunt", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share a caption that made you want to buy something."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Copywriting and persuasive messaging", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn how to write hooks, benefits, calls to action and honest persuasive captions."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Write your campaign captions", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write three captions for your calendar posts using AIDA or PAS."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Customer engagement and conversion", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn how to turn likes, comments and messages into orders and repeat customers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Role-play - from enquiry to order", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Practise handling a customer message from the first question to a confirmed order."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Search engine optimisation fundamentals and Google Business Profile", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn how search works and how a small business gets found on Google Search and Maps."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Keyword list and Google Business Profile draft", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Find keywords for your business and draft its Google Business Profile."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Answer questions on copywriting, conversion and search."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What does AIDA stand for?"}, {"question": "Turn the feature 'delivery within 2 hours' into a benefit."}, {"question": "A customer asks 'how much?' in a comment. What should you do?"}, {"question": "Name three parts of a Google Business Profile that help a business get found."}, {"question": "Why should you never buy fake reviews?"}], "objectives": ["Write captions using the AIDA and Problem-Agitate-Solution structures, with a clear call to action", "Turn product features into customer benefits", "Write honest marketing copy that avoids false or misleading claims", "Use engagement and conversion techniques (fast replies, social proof, clear offers, easy ordering and payment)", "Handle an enquiry from first message to confirmed order in a role-play", "Explain how search engines rank results and the basics of keywords and local SEO", "Draft a Google Business Profile and a keyword list for their business"], "track_project": "A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission.", "expected_outputs": ["Three finished captions with hooks and calls to action", "Enquiry-to-order conversation script (from the role-play)", "Keyword list (ten phrases, five priority)", "Google Business Profile draft"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Enquiry-to-order role-play", "instructions": "In pairs, handle a scenario-card customer message from first question to confirmed order, then swap."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Features to benefits", "instructions": "Turn three product features into customer benefits for your persona."}, {"title": "Keyword hunt", "instructions": "Use Google autocomplete to find ten real search phrases and pick the five most relevant."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Content Creation teaches captions as creative writing and storytelling for audiences. Digital Marketing teaches copy as persuasion with a measurable action (an enquiry, an order, a booking).", "Entrepreneurship & Business covers sales and negotiation face to face and in business deals; here selling happens in online conversations."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Three campaign captions", "instructions": "Write one AIDA, one PAS and one short status caption for your calendar, each with a hook and one CTA."}, {"title": "Google Business Profile draft", "instructions": "Complete the profile template with a keyword-rich but natural description."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"bac17027-f1f6-4d94-a1a5-b77b0bde6347","session_id":"3b443948-c867-4209-810d-8a50039a8db5","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Canva (free) or a similar free design tool; paper and coloured pens as a backup", "Brand mini-kit template and content calendar template (Google Sheets or paper)", "Meta Business Suite (for showing scheduling)", "Real product photos from the business (with permission)"], "topics": [{"title": "Branding and brand identity", "subtopics": ["Brand versus logo", "Brand promise", "Brand personality and voice", "Colours, fonts and imagery", "Consistency builds recognition and trust"]}, {"title": "Content planning", "subtopics": ["Content pillars", "Content mix (educate, entertain, inspire, sell, community)", "Posting frequency that is realistic", "Using the persona to choose topics"]}, {"title": "Content calendars", "subtopics": ["Calendar columns (date, platform, format, pillar, idea, caption hook, call to action, status)", "Planning around local dates (paydays, holidays, back to school, exams, festive season)", "Batching content"]}, {"title": "Marketing graphics and visual communication", "subtopics": ["Visual hierarchy", "Contrast and readability on small phone screens", "Alignment and white space", "Using real product photos", "Templates without looking generic", "Image sizes for each platform"]}], "purpose": "Learners give their campaign a consistent look and voice, turn their persona and platform plan into a two-week content calendar with clear content pillars, and design their first marketing graphics in a free design tool using visual communication principles.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Shiru's Bakes brand mini-kit and content pillars.", "A Mombasa juice vendor planning posts around hot afternoons and Friday prayers footfall.", "A school uniform seller in Nyeri planning a content calendar around school opening dates.", "A Nakuru youth football club using consistent colours and logo on all match-day posters."], "homework": ["Finish both graphics and design one more for your calendar.", "Take at least five real photos of the business or product (with permission) for the next session's posts. Ask before including any person's face, and do not photograph children. On the simulated option, use your own product photos or free-licence images.", "Show your brand kit and one graphic to the business owner and note their feedback."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and what businesses post", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share what you noticed about the businesses you followed."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Branding and brand identity", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn what a brand is and the parts of a simple brand identity."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Create your brand mini-kit", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write a brand promise, personality, voice and choose colours and fonts for your campaign business."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Content pillars and the content calendar", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn how to plan content around pillars and organise it in a calendar."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Build your two-week content calendar", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Fill a two-week calendar for your campaign with at least six planned posts."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Visual communication principles", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn the design principles that make marketing graphics clear on a phone screen."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Demonstration and practice - designing marketing graphics", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Watch a marketing graphic designed in Canva, then design two of your own."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Peer review and knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Test each other's graphics with the 3-second test and answer the knowledge check."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "What is the difference between a brand and a logo?"}, {"question": "Write a brand promise for your business in one sentence."}, {"question": "What are content pillars and why use them?"}, {"question": "Name three visual communication principles."}, {"question": "Your calendar has 10 posts and 8 are sales posts. What is the problem?"}], "objectives": ["Explain branding and brand identity (name, promise, personality, voice, colours, fonts, logo) with local examples", "Create a brand mini-kit for their campaign business", "Define three or four content pillars and a content mix for their persona", "Build a two-week content calendar with dates, platforms, formats, captions ideas and goals", "Apply visual communication principles (hierarchy, contrast, alignment, white space, readable text) to a marketing graphic", "Design two marketing graphics in Canva (or a similar free tool) that follow the brand mini-kit"], "track_project": "A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission.", "expected_outputs": ["Brand mini-kit (promise, personality, voice, colours, fonts, photo style)", "Content pillars and a two-week content calendar", "Two branded marketing graphics (feed post and status or story)"], "group_activities": [{"title": "3-second test", "instructions": "Show your graphic to a partner for three seconds; they say what it means; improve it if they miss the message."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Content pillars", "instructions": "Write three or four content pillars for your business, each linked to a persona need."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Content Creation teaches graphic design and visual storytelling in depth (layout, typography, carousels, video). Digital Marketing uses design only as far as needed to communicate an offer and a call to action that serve the campaign goal.", "AI Tools covers AI-generated images; learners in Digital Marketing may use AI help only with disclosure and must prefer real product photos."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Brand mini-kit", "instructions": "Complete the brand promise, personality, voice examples, colours, fonts and photo style for your campaign business."}, {"title": "Two-week content calendar", "instructions": "Plan at least six posts and two WhatsApp status or broadcast items with pillar, format, idea, hook and call to action."}, {"title": "Two marketing graphics", "instructions": "Design one feed post and one status or story graphic that follow your brand kit and pass the 3-second test."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"c59e41ba-d40b-4f1d-8498-57a834815b65","session_id":"73a7a9f1-7e68-4ef7-ad69-a6eb5e907a7d","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Projector or screen sharing with sound, learners' portfolios", "Showcase rubric, feedback cards or DIRA LEARN, timer, 30-day plan template", "Low-data showcase: play every video from a downloaded file; online learners share their link or file in advance."], "topics": [{"title": "Presenting creative work", "subtopics": ["Showcase structure", "Explaining creative choices", "Answering questions"]}, {"title": "Pitching yourself and your work", "subtopics": ["Creator self-pitch", "Sharing your portfolio link professionally", "Talking about rates and briefs"]}, {"title": "Professional behaviour and workplace readiness", "subtopics": ["Showcase etiquette", "Receiving critical feedback", "Following up with clients and guests", "Pathways - employment, freelance, creator brand, media and NGO communications"]}, {"title": "Next steps", "subtopics": ["30-day content plan", "Staying consistent"]}], "purpose": "Learners present their creative project and portfolio, explain their creative and ethical choices, and answer questions. They practise pitching themselves as creators to clients and employers, show professional behaviour in a showcase setting and leave with a 30-day content plan.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A Nyeri creator pitching cafes with a 12,000-view video as proof.", "A Kilifi youth media team showing a conservation clean-up documentary.", "A Kisumu photographer using the portfolio to apply for an NGO communications internship."], "homework": ["Submit the final project, your portfolio (restricted-access link or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN) and the 30-day plan in DIRA LEARN.", "Thank your brief owner and deliver the final files."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Showcase set-up and expectations", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Project presentations and questions - block 1", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The first six presenters in each room present and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Project presentations and questions - block 2", "minutes": 30, "summary": "The next six presenters in each room present; recorded presenters answer their live questions."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Feedback round", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share feedback and learn from patterns across projects."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Pitching yourself and workplace readiness", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Practise pitching yourself as a creator and learn the pathways for creative work."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "30-day content plan", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Write your plan for the next 30 days of content."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Celebration and close", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Celebrate and learn what happens next."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Presentation scored with the content showcase rubric."}, {"question": "Explain one creative choice and one responsible-content choice in your project."}, {"question": "Deliver your creator pitch."}], "objectives": ["Present the creative project and portfolio clearly in three minutes", "Explain at least one creative choice and one responsible-content choice", "Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric", "Pitch themselves as a creator with the portfolio as proof", "Write a 30-day content plan"], "track_project": "A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Portfolio submissions for the programme use a restricted-access link shared only with named reviewers (for example a Google Drive folder set to \"Restricted\" and shared with the facilitator's email) or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN - never a public or \"anyone with the link\" link. A public portfolio is the learner's own optional choice, after consent checks. Privacy option: the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log.", "expected_outputs": ["Presented creative project and portfolio (final project) with rubric score and feedback", "Portfolio message and creator pitch", "30-day content plan", "Written feedback given to two peers"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Peer feedback", "instructions": "Give rubric-based written feedback to two assigned presenters."}, {"title": "Pitch practice", "instructions": "Practise your creator pitch in pairs."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Portfolio message", "instructions": "Write a three-sentence message introducing yourself and your portfolio link to a potential client."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Every track ends with the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment; the Content Creation rubric weights storytelling, production quality and responsible content."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Showcase presentation", "instructions": "Present your project and portfolio in three minutes and answer questions for two minutes."}, {"title": "30-day content plan", "instructions": "Complete the plan with goal, pillars, rhythm, weekly actions, measure, partner and risk."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"d879fd44-128f-495d-b3c2-533a2620b21e","session_id":"8a5cdcac-0981-4a47-9e37-f929a17941e5","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Free mobile video editor (for example CapCut, InShot or VN - check current terms and availability)", "Earphones; learners' footage; facilitator's sample footage as a backup", "Editing checklist and caption template", "Free editors: CapCut, InShot or VN (check current terms) or the phone's built-in editor. Low storage: edit a 30-second version. Low data: download the editor and any music on programme Wi-Fi and export at 720p."], "topics": [{"title": "Video editing", "subtopics": ["Hook first - put the best moment at the start", "Cut on action and remove dead time", "Pacing and rhythm", "Jump cuts and sequences", "Transitions - less is more", "Speed changes", "Colour and brightness basics", "Aspect ratio and safe zones"]}, {"title": "Audio and sound (editing)", "subtopics": ["Balancing voice and music levels", "Noise reduction", "Recording a voice-over", "Sound effects", "Music licensing - platform libraries, royalty-free and licensed music", "Why using popular songs without permission can get content removed or muted"]}, {"title": "Captions and creative writing", "subtopics": ["On-screen captions for accessibility and silent viewing", "Checking auto-captions (names, Kiswahili, Sheng)", "Writing post captions - hook, value, story, call to action", "Hashtags and keywords", "Voice and tone"]}], "purpose": "Learners turn raw footage into a finished short video. They learn editing principles and practise them in a free mobile editor, improve sound and use music legally, write on-screen captions for accessibility and post captions that connect, and export correctly for each platform.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["A 40-second 'mandazi at 6 am in Kariokor' video with a voice-over in Kiswahili and English captions.", "A Mombasa creator whose video was muted for using a popular song, re-uploaded with platform-library music.", "Auto-captions turning Kenyan place names into nonsense, corrected by hand."], "homework": ["Improve your video based on the screening feedback.", "Collect your logo, colours or style references for your brief (or start thinking about your creator brand).", "Publish your video on your own account or the brief's account (with permission) and note views, watch time and comments after 48 hours. Private option: share it through a restricted link with named people only, or submit it only in DIRA LEARN, and collect feedback from three people instead of public views."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and footage check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Check your footage is ready and learn today's plan."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Editing principles and demonstration", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn the editing principles and watch a short video edited step by step."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Edit your video", "minutes": 25, "summary": "Edit your 30 to 60-second video using the principles."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Audio and sound - editing and legal music", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Improve sound, add a voice-over and use music legally."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Captions and creative writing", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Add accurate on-screen captions and write a post caption that connects."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Finish and export", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Finish your video and export it correctly for your platform."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Screening and knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Screen a few videos and give feedback."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Why should the best moment come first in a short video?"}, {"question": "What does 'cut on action' mean?"}, {"question": "Why add on-screen captions?"}, {"question": "Where can you get music you are allowed to use?"}, {"question": "What export settings are a safe default for short vertical video?"}], "objectives": ["Apply editing principles (hook first, cut on action, pacing, jump cuts, sequences, minimal transitions)", "Edit a 30 to 60-second video in a free mobile editor (trim, split, arrange, speed, text, audio, colour)", "Improve audio (levels, noise reduction, voice-over) and use music legally (platform libraries, royalty-free, licensed)", "Add accurate on-screen captions for accessibility and check auto-captions", "Write a post caption using creative writing techniques with a hook and call to action", "Export with the right settings for each platform"], "track_project": "A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Portfolio submissions for the programme use a restricted-access link shared only with named reviewers (for example a Google Drive folder set to \"Restricted\" and shared with the facilitator's email) or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN - never a public or \"anyone with the link\" link. A public portfolio is the learner's own optional choice, after consent checks. Privacy option: the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log.", "expected_outputs": ["Edited 30 to 60-second video with captions and legal audio", "Post caption with hook and call to action"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Screening", "instructions": "Watch volunteers' videos and give feedback on hook, pacing, sound and captions."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Find the hook", "instructions": "Look through your clips and choose the strongest 2-second moment to open your video."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Digital Marketing covers persuasive captions and copywriting tied to a sale. Content Creation covers captions as accessibility and creative writing that builds an audience."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Edited short video", "instructions": "Edit a 30 to 60-second video with a strong hook, no dead time, balanced audio, corrected captions and a call to action."}, {"title": "Post caption", "instructions": "Write a caption with a hook, value or story, call to action and relevant hashtags."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"db9739d5-432d-4699-b7c6-5222d2e4fec3","session_id":"708f0f85-2909-443e-b47e-314a2934f2ea","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["At least one free AI chat assistant available in Kenya", "AI task log template and scenario cards", "A public, non-personal article or notice for the summarising demo", "Shared document or flip chart for the group AI use code", "Free and low-data options: the free tier of an AI assistant in a phone browser (no paid plan or app needed); text chat uses little data; one shared phone or laptop per pair or three; printed scenario cards."], "topics": [{"title": "Understanding artificial intelligence", "subtopics": ["What AI is and is not", "Machine learning - learning patterns from examples", "Generative AI and large language models in simple terms", "Why AI can be confidently wrong (hallucinations)", "AI already around us in Kenya"]}, {"title": "Types and uses of AI tools", "subtopics": ["Chat assistants (ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, Claude, Meta AI)", "Image and design tools", "Voice, transcription and translation", "Research tools that show sources", "AI inside everyday apps (email, documents, WhatsApp, Canva)", "Coding and data assistants", "Free versus paid versions and data limits"]}, {"title": "Responsible and ethical AI use", "subtopics": ["Honesty and disclosure", "Academic and workplace integrity", "Privacy and personal data", "Accuracy and verification", "Fairness and bias", "Deepfakes and misuse", "Humans stay responsible for decisions"]}], "purpose": "Learners build an accurate mental model of AI - what it is, how today's generative tools produce answers and why they make mistakes - and survey the main types of AI tools. They try three practical tasks, record what needed human checking, and agree a group code for responsible and ethical AI use that applies for the rest of the track.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Fraud alerts on mobile money and bank transactions as everyday machine learning.", "A Kitengela hardware shop using an AI-drafted, owner-checked set of replies to common customer questions.", "A university student in Eldoret using AI to explain a statistics concept and then checking it against lecture notes.", "Viral AI-generated fake images and voice notes shared on Kenyan social media, and why checking matters."], "homework": ["Use AI for one more real task this week and add it to your task log with your checks. Do not paste personal data (ID numbers, phone numbers, health details) or a business's private information into an AI tool without permission.", "Find one example of AI in a Kenyan service you use and explain in two sentences what it does.", "Think of a real person, business or group you could help with an AI-assisted solution for your final project."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Welcome to AI Tools and track overview", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Meet your track group and see the six sessions and final AI project."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "What AI is and how it works", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Understand AI, machine learning and generative AI in plain language, and why AI makes mistakes."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Types and uses of AI tools", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Survey the main families of AI tools and what each is good for."}, {"kind": "demonstration", "title": "Demonstration - three AI tasks and what needed checking", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Watch an AI assistant explain, draft and summarise, and see the facilitator check each output."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Your three AI tasks", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Complete three AI-assisted tasks of your own and fill in the AI task log."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Responsible AI scenarios and group AI use code", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Discuss real ethical dilemmas and agree how the group will use AI responsibly."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Knowledge check", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Answer quick questions on how AI works and responsible use."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "In simple words, how does a chat assistant produce an answer?"}, {"question": "What is a hallucination?"}, {"question": "Name three types of AI tools and one use for each."}, {"question": "Give two examples of information you must never put into an AI tool."}, {"question": "Write your disclosure line for a piece of work AI helped with."}], "objectives": ["Explain in plain words what AI, machine learning and generative AI are, and how a chat assistant produces an answer", "Identify AI already used in everyday Kenyan life (spam filters, maps, fraud alerts, translation, customer-care chatbots)", "Name at least six types of AI tools and a practical use for each", "Complete three AI-assisted tasks and record what the learner checked or changed", "Explain five principles of responsible AI use (honesty, privacy, accuracy, fairness, human responsibility)", "Agree and sign a group AI use code"], "track_project": "An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent.", "expected_outputs": ["AI task log with three completed tasks and checks", "Signed group AI use code with the disclosure line format"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Responsible AI scenarios", "instructions": "Discuss an ethical scenario, identify harms and the responsible choice, and report back."}, {"title": "Group AI use code", "instructions": "Agree six to eight rules for responsible AI use, including a disclosure line, and sign it."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Tool matching", "instructions": "Match five everyday tasks to the right family of AI tool and say why."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Core session 2 introduced AI awareness for everyone. Here learners study how AI works and the full range of tools, which the rest of the track builds on."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "AI task log", "instructions": "Complete three real AI-assisted tasks (explain, draft, summarise) and record prompt, quality, checks, changes and decision."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"dd3c5d4e-f790-4a88-85cc-564ea976c999","session_id":"ae6f04a8-ca62-4c3d-a398-60e98fbac35d","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["All tools from the track (phone, editor, design tool)", "Portfolio checklist, case-study template, simple client brief template", "Free portfolio platforms: a Google Drive folder with \"Restricted\" sharing (named people only), or a single PDF or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN, for the programme submission; Behance (free) only if the learner chooses a public portfolio later. Compress videos to 720p to save data."], "topics": [{"title": "Portfolio development", "subtopics": ["What clients and employers look for", "Quality over quantity - three to five best pieces", "Showing variety (video, photo, design)", "Case-study notes", "Portfolio platforms", "Keeping the credit and consent log with the portfolio"]}, {"title": "Working with clients (introduction)", "subtopics": ["Brief and deliverables", "Revisions and deadlines", "Simple pricing ideas and agreeing payment before work", "Delivering files professionally"]}, {"title": "Creative project completion", "subtopics": ["Finishing production", "Quality check", "Peer testing", "Revising"]}], "purpose": "Learners pull their work together into a professional content portfolio and complete their final creative project for a real brief. They learn what clients and employers look for, write short case-study notes, finish production, test with peers and prepare their showcase presentation.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["The 'Otieno Shoots' portfolio with a food video, a photo set and a carousel, each with a short case study.", "A Nyeri creator who got a paid brief from a local cafe after sharing a Google Drive portfolio link.", "A youth group media team in Kilifi documenting a beach clean-up for a conservation organisation."], "homework": ["Finish your portfolio and project and upload them to DIRA LEARN.", "Practise your showcase presentation twice within three minutes.", "Ask your brief owner for a short comment on the work (a testimonial) if they are happy to give one.", "If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute project and portfolio presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Project check-in", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Confirm your brief, your best pieces and what is left to make."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "What makes a strong portfolio", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn how to present your work so clients and employers take you seriously."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Creative project production", "minutes": 45, "summary": "Finish every piece of your creative project and build your portfolio."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "group", "title": "Peer testing", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Test your portfolio and project with peers using the checklist."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Revise and prepare the showcase", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Make improvements and prepare your three-minute showcase."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Showcase briefing and homework", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Learn the showcase format and what to submit."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Portfolio checked with the portfolio checklist."}, {"question": "What should you agree in writing with a client before starting work?"}], "objectives": ["Explain what makes a strong content portfolio (best three to five pieces, variety, results, story behind each piece)", "Choose a portfolio platform (Google Drive folder, Behance, Instagram highlights, a simple website), and submit it through a restricted-access link or DIRA LEARN", "Write a short case study for one piece (brief, idea, process, result, learning)", "Complete the creative project to a publishable standard", "Improve the project based on peer testing", "Explain basic freelance terms (brief, deliverables, revisions, deadline, rate) in simple language"], "track_project": "A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Portfolio submissions for the programme use a restricted-access link shared only with named reviewers (for example a Google Drive folder set to \"Restricted\" and shared with the facilitator's email) or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN - never a public or \"anyone with the link\" link. A public portfolio is the learner's own optional choice, after consent checks. Privacy option: the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log.", "expected_outputs": ["Completed creative project (video, photo set, branded designs)", "Content portfolio with three to five pieces, case-study notes and contact details", "Completed credit and consent log", "Showcase presentation draft"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Portfolio peer test", "instructions": "Show your portfolio to two peers, who use the checklist to give two strengths and two improvements."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Case study", "instructions": "Write a case study for your best piece - brief, idea, process, result, learning."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Every track builds its final project in session 7. The Content Creation project is judged on storytelling, production quality and responsible content use."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Creative project completion", "instructions": "Finish every piece of your creative project to a publishable standard."}, {"title": "Portfolio build", "instructions": "Build a portfolio with three to five best pieces, case-study notes, contact details and the credit and consent log. Submit it for the programme only as a restricted-access link shared only with named reviewers (for example a Google Drive folder set to \"Restricted\" and shared with the facilitator's email) or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN, not as a public or \"anyone with the link\" link."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}, {"id":"f2b43cff-3698-480a-8544-466cfee37ad4","session_id":"8a1c572a-22f6-4d92-9408-cf320f22f655","version":"V13.2","content":{"tools": ["Smartphones with free storage, earphones with a mic, optional cheap tripod or lapel mic", "White manila or foil reflector, a plain cloth background", "Shot checklist", "Low-cost kit: any smartphone camera; a stack of books or a cup as a tripod; white manila or foil as a reflector; a small room with curtains or clothes for clearer sound; learners without a camera phone shoot in pairs or threes."], "topics": [{"title": "Photography using smartphones", "subtopics": ["Clean the lens", "Gridlines and focus/exposure lock", "Portrait mode and HDR", "Avoid digital zoom - move closer", "Taking many shots and choosing the best"]}, {"title": "Lighting and composition", "subtopics": ["Rule of thirds", "Leading lines and framing", "Angles - eye level, high, low", "Clean backgrounds", "Natural and window light", "Golden hour and harsh midday light", "DIY reflectors and diffusers"]}, {"title": "Mobile video production", "subtopics": ["Vertical versus horizontal", "Shot types - wide, medium, close-up", "B-roll", "Stable shots (two hands, lean on something, cheap tripod)", "Hold each shot for 5 seconds", "Simple movement - pan, push in, follow"]}, {"title": "Audio and sound (capture)", "subtopics": ["Why audio matters more than picture", "Quiet locations", "Mic distance", "Wired earphone and lapel mics", "Wind, echo and background noise", "Recording a test clip"]}], "purpose": "Learners master the phone as a camera. They learn composition rules, how to use natural light, camera settings that matter, shot types for video, stable filming and clean sound capture, then shoot a photo set and the clips for their storyboard.", "version": "V13.2", "examples": ["Food photos at a Kisumu fish spot using window light and a top-down angle.", "A Nairobi tailor's hands as a close-up B-roll.", "A Marikiti market wide shot using leading lines of the stalls.", "Interviewing a youth group leader in a quiet church hall instead of beside a busy road."], "homework": ["Complete any missing storyboard clips and reshoot the shot you chose.", "Back up your footage and install a free editor (for example CapCut, InShot or VN; check the app's current terms).", "Take five more photos using golden-hour light."], "segments": [{"kind": "introduction", "title": "Recap and hooks you found", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Share the hooks you noticed and check your shot list."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Composition and phone camera basics", "minutes": 20, "summary": "Learn composition techniques and the camera settings that make the biggest difference."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Lighting", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Use natural light to make photos and videos look professional."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Mobile video production", "minutes": 15, "summary": "Learn shot types, B-roll, stable filming and simple camera movement."}, {"kind": "break", "title": "Stretch break", "minutes": 5, "summary": "A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."}, {"kind": "lesson", "title": "Capturing clean sound", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Record clear voice and avoid the most common sound problems."}, {"kind": "exercise", "title": "Practical shoot", "minutes": 30, "summary": "Shoot a five-photo set and the clips for your storyboard."}, {"kind": "assessment", "title": "Peer review of shots", "minutes": 10, "summary": "Review each other's photos and clips with the shot checklist."}, {"kind": "conclusion", "title": "Wrap-up and homework", "minutes": 5, "summary": "Summarise and confirm the homework."}], "assessment": [{"question": "Explain the rule of thirds."}, {"question": "Your subject is standing with a bright window behind them and their face is dark. What do you do?"}, {"question": "Name the three basic shot types and what each shows."}, {"question": "Why should you avoid digital zoom?"}, {"question": "Name two ways to improve sound when filming on a phone."}], "objectives": ["Apply composition techniques (rule of thirds, leading lines, framing, angles, clean backgrounds, symmetry)", "Use phone camera features (gridlines, focus and exposure lock, portrait mode, HDR) and avoid digital zoom", "Use natural light well (window light, golden hour, avoiding harsh midday sun, simple reflectors)", "Film stable shots in the right orientation using wide, medium and close-up shots and B-roll", "Capture clean audio (quiet location, mic close to the speaker, avoiding wind and echo)", "Shoot a five-photo set and the clips for their storyboard, with consent"], "track_project": "A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Portfolio submissions for the programme use a restricted-access link shared only with named reviewers (for example a Google Drive folder set to \"Restricted\" and shared with the facilitator's email) or files uploaded in DIRA LEARN - never a public or \"anyone with the link\" link. A public portfolio is the learner's own optional choice, after consent checks. Privacy option: the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log.", "expected_outputs": ["Five-photo set for the brief", "Storyboard clips including B-roll and a clean audio clip (complete or in progress)", "Peer-reviewed shot checklist with a reshoot plan"], "group_activities": [{"title": "Shoot pairs", "instructions": "Work in pairs to shoot, hold reflectors and check each other's framing and sound."}, {"title": "Shot checklist review", "instructions": "Review your partner's photos and clip with the checklist and choose one shot to reshoot."}], "guided_exercises": [{"title": "Composition hunt", "instructions": "Take one photo for each technique - rule of thirds, leading lines, framing, low angle, top-down."}], "cross_track_notes": ["Digital Marketing asks learners to take real product photos for campaigns; Content Creation teaches the full photography and video craft behind them."], "individual_activities": [{"title": "Five-photo set", "instructions": "Shoot wide, medium, close-up, angle and portrait photos for your brief using good light and composition."}, {"title": "Storyboard clips", "instructions": "Film the clips on your shot list, including three B-roll shots and one clean audio clip."}]},"status":"draft","approved_by":null,"approved_at":null,"updated_by":null,"updated_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00","created_at":"2026-11-21T19:24:48.274223+03:00"}]$dira_json$::jsonb); alter table public.curriculum_lesson_plans enable trigger user;
